module hc.selfhost.codegen;

// The self-hosted codegen, phase 1 -- walks the checked `FnDecl`/`Stmt`/`Expr` AST and drives
// real ASM (`ClassWriter`/`MethodVisitor`/`Label`) to emit a real, loadable, runnable `.class`
// file. Builds directly on the ASM-interop spike from earlier this session (verified then: HC
// calling into ASM's builder API works cleanly with zero new compiler features) and the same
// `byte[]`-avoiding shim pattern that spike proved out for the one step HC genuinely can't do
// itself (`ClassWriter.toByteArray()` -- see `IDEAS.md`'s `Byte` primitive entry).
//
// **String locals now supported**: since the simplified `Expr`/`Stmt` AST (`Ast.hc`) has no
// mutable per-node `ty` field the way the real compiler's own `Expr.ty` does, `gen_expr`/
// `gen_binary`/`gen_unary` now each *return* the "Int"/"Bool"/"String" type tag of whatever they
// just pushed, computed inline during the same walk that emits bytecode -- mirroring exactly how
// `Checker.hotc`'s own `check_expr` computes `Ty` in one pass. That tag drives `ALOAD`/`ASTORE`
// vs `ILOAD`/`ISTORE` for locals, `String.concat` vs `IADD` for `+`, and `Object.equals` vs
// `IF_ICMPEQ`/`NE` for `==`/`!=` -- all real bugs before this (String locals/comparisons/`+`
// silently got Int-only bytecode).
//
// **Param/return types now respected too**: `gen_fn` builds each function's real JVM descriptor
// from its *declared* param/return type names (`fn_descriptor`/`descriptor_of_type`) instead of
// always assuming `Int`, seeds `var_types` for each param from its real declared type (not a
// hardcoded default), and `Call` codegen now looks up the real callee descriptor from `fn_descs`
// (built once per `compile_program` from every fn's real signature) instead of guessing
// `(I...I)I` from the argument count -- so a `String`-param or `String`-returning function
// (previously silently miscompiled) now generates correct bytecode. `known_fns` (a plain
// membership set) was folded into `fn_descs` -- a callee not present there is exactly the "not
// defined in the same generated class" case, same clear `CodegenError` as before.
//
// **`MethodCall` now supports built-in `String` methods**: `length`/`toUpperCase`/`toLowerCase`/
// `trim`/`contains`/`indexOf` compile to real `INVOKEVIRTUAL java/lang/String.*` calls. Nothing
// else -- there is no struct/class/method table in this AST subset at all (see `Ast.hc`'s own
// header), so a small fixed set of real `String` methods is the only thing safely codegen-able;
// anything else throws a clear `CodegenError`.
//
// **Structs now real**: each `StructDecl` becomes its own real, separate `.class` file
// (`gen_struct`) -- public fields plus a constructor taking every field value in declared order
// and `PUTFIELD`-ing each. `StructLit` codegen (`NEW`+`DUP`+push fields in *declared* order,
// resolved by name from the literal's own possibly-different order+`INVOKESPECIAL <init>`),
// `FieldAccess` (`GETFIELD`), and `FieldAssign` (`PUTFIELD`, `DUP_X1`-ing the value first so the
// assignment still behaves as a value-producing expression like plain `Assign` does) all now
// generate real bytecode. The `is_ref_type`/`ALOAD`/`ASTORE` local-variable dispatch that used to
// special-case `String` now treats *any* non-`Int`/`Bool` type as a reference (struct instances
// need `ALOAD`/`ASTORE` exactly like `String` does) -- one rule covers both instead of two.
// `==`/`!=` between two struct-typed operands now compiles to `IF_ACMPEQ`/`NE` (reference
// identity -- there is no per-field structural equality here, matching the real compiler's own
// still-open structural-equality gap noted in `IDEAS.md`).
//
// **Nested struct-typed fields verified**: `Line { start: Point, end: Point }` compiled and ran
// correctly with zero code changes needed -- the generic `descriptor_of_type` fallback
// (`"L" + name + ";"` for anything not `Int`/`Bool`/`String`/`Unit`) and the general
// `is_ref_type`-based `ALOAD`/`GETFIELD` dispatch already covered it, since a struct field is
// handled the same way a fn param or local of that type is everywhere else in this file.
//
// **Struct methods / `impl` blocks now real**: `impl Name { fn method(self[, ...]) { ... } }`
// becomes a real instance method (`ACC_PUBLIC`, not static) on the struct's own class --
// `self`/`&self`/`&mut self` (parsed but not distinguished, see `Parser.hotc`'s own header) all
// land in the always-implicit JVM local slot 0, exactly like any real instance method's receiver.
// `MethodCall` codegen checks the user-defined method table (`struct_methods`, keyed
// `"StructName#method"`) before falling back to the built-in `String` methods above -- so a
// struct method and `String`'s own methods share the same call syntax and dispatch correctly.
// `gen_fn`/`gen_method` now share their cross-function/cross-struct lookup setup via
// `build_codegen`; `build_program_info` rebuilds a whole `ProgramInfo` fresh from the program's
// real `fns`/`structs`/`impls` (passed *borrowed*, `&Vec<...>`, specifically so calling it many
// times across a codegen loop -- once per fn, once per method -- never risks consuming them).
//
// **Enums / `match` now real**: each `EnumDecl` becomes one real `.class` file (`gen_enum`) with
// a `tag: Int` field plus the *union* of every variant's fields (same simplification `Checker
// .hotc`'s `register_enum` makes -- field lookups don't distinguish which variant a field
// belongs to). Variant construction reuses `StructLit` codegen (`gen_expr` checks `variant_owner`
// first): `NEW`+trivial `INVOKESPECIAL <init>`, then individual `PUTFIELD`s for `tag` and each
// given field (not one big constructor call like a real struct -- different variants populate
// different subsets of the enum's unioned fields). `match` codegen (`gen_stmt`) stores the
// subject in a raw, unregistered local slot, then chains `GETFIELD tag` + `IF_ICMPNE` per arm,
// binding each arm's fields via `GETFIELD` into fresh locals before its body.
//
// **Real bug found and fixed**: a fn/method body that returns on every path (the checker doesn't
// verify this, but every demo here does) still left `end_label` (or `match`'s own end label) with
// literally no instruction after it -- sitting exactly at the end of the method's bytecode. The
// JVM verifier rejects that outright (a stack-map frame must describe a real instruction offset).
// `gen_fallback_return` now unconditionally emits one dummy return after every real body -- dead
// code whenever the body already returned everywhere, but a genuine instruction at that trailing
// offset either way. Caught via a real `VerifyError`/`ClassFormatError` on `Shape`'s exhaustive
// 3-arm `match`, not by inspection. (A related but separate bug fixed alongside it: several
// `ClassWriter.visit`/`visitMethod`/`visitField` calls passed `""` for the *generic signature*
// param instead of a real `null` -- HC's `null` literal can't be passed as a bare call argument,
// so `CodegenShim` gained `visitClassHeader`/`visitMethodNoSig` shims for it. `""` there produces
// a genuinely malformed Signature attribute per the JVM spec -- harmless to the real verifier,
// which never reads Signature attributes, but it crashed `javap`.)
//
// **Generics now real**: a single-type-parameter generic struct (`struct Box<T> { value: T }`)
// gets monomorphized *on demand* -- `Checker.hotc`'s `ensure_instantiated` substitutes the type
// parameter the first time a concrete name like `"Box$Int"` is actually used and registers it as
// an ordinary struct; `compile_program` reads the resulting list back (`checker.mono_needed`) and
// emits one real `.class` file per distinct instantiation (`monomorphized_fields` redoes the same
// substitution here, since this file doesn't share the checker's own `GenericTemplate`
// representation, only the plain `structs: Vec<StructDecl>` both sides already have). Everywhere
// else -- field access, `descriptor_of_type`, `is_ref_type` -- treats `"Box$Int"` exactly like any
// other struct name, no special-casing needed beyond that one substitution step. Real bug found by
// this, unrelated to generics but only surfaced by it: `Checker.hotc`'s `vars` was never reset
// between checking different functions, so two functions sharing a param name with *different*
// types had the second silently shadowed by the first's leftover type (fixed in `check_fn`/
// `check_method`).
//
// **Generic enums now real too** (`enum Option<T> { ... }`, same `"Base$Arg"` monomorphization
// convention as generic structs): a mono name might be a generic struct's OR a generic enum's
// instantiation -- `is_generic_enum_mono` (searching `enums` for a `type_param: Some` decl whose
// name matches the mono name's base) tells the two apart, routing `compile_program`'s emission
// loop and `build_program_info`'s field-table building to `monomorphized_enum_variants`/`gen_enum`
// (substituted variants, reusing `gen_enum` completely unchanged -- it already just takes a name
// and a variant list) instead of `monomorphized_fields`/`gen_struct`. `gen_expr`'s own `StructLit`
// codegen resolves a qualified construction site's owner class directly from `Expr::StructLit`'s
// new `generic_base` field (set only for the explicit `Base<Arg>::Variant { ... }` form -- see
// `Ast.hc`'s own `EnumDecl` header) rather than a bare-name `variant_owner` lookup, which would
// otherwise collide across multiple concrete instantiations of the same generic enum used in one
// program. `Match` codegen itself needed NO changes at all -- it already keys every lookup
// (`variant_tags`, `field_type_of`) off the subject's own concrete runtime type name (`sty`), never
// a bare variant name, so it was already correct for generic enum instances with zero new code.
//
// **`extern class` now real**: constructors and static methods (`Alias::new(...)`,
// `Alias::method(...)`, parsed as `Expr::StaticCall`) and instance methods (plain
// `Expr::MethodCall`, checked against the extern method table before falling through to struct
// methods/`String` builtins) all compile to real `NEW`/`INVOKESPECIAL`/`INVOKESTATIC`/
// `INVOKEVIRTUAL` against the class's real binary name. Scope cut, disclosed: extern *method*
// params (the methods declared inside an `extern class` block itself) must still be
// `Int`/`Bool`/`String`, a return type one of those, `Unit`, or the literal `Self` (mapped to the
// real binary name's own descriptor) -- and `binary_name` must already be JVM-internal-slash form
// (`"java/io/File"`, no dot-to-slash conversion). Verified with a real `java.lang.StringBuilder`
// (constructor + fluent `Self`-returning instance methods) and a real static `java.lang.Math.max`
// call.
//
// **Extern-typed struct fields and plain fn/method params/returns now real** (closes the gap
// disclosed just above): a struct field, or a top-level fn/impl-method param or return type, may
// now name an extern class alias (e.g. `struct Wrapper { sb: JSB, tag: Int }`,
// `fn build_it(sb: JSB) -> Wrapper`) and resolves to the real binary name's descriptor
// (`descriptor_of_type`/`fn_descriptor`, threaded a parallel `alias_names`/`alias_binaries` pair
// built once per `build_program_info`/`gen_struct` call from `externs`; `gen_expr`'s own
// `CodeGen`-method call sites use `self.resolve_descriptor`/`self.resolve_elem_class_name` instead,
// resolving via `self.extern_binary: Registry<String>` since they don't have the parallel `Vec`s in
// scope). Scope cut, still disclosed: enum fields do not support extern types this round (always
// resolved with empty alias vecs in `gen_enum`), and extern *method* params/returns (see above)
// stay restricted to `Int`/`Bool`/`String`/`Unit`/`Self`. Verified with a real
// `java.lang.StringBuilder`-typed struct field, constructed via a fn taking that extern type as a
// param, read back through a fn returning `String` from a method call on the extern-typed field.
//
// **Scope cut, still disclosed**: no static methods in `impl` blocks (every method needs a
// `self`), no operator overloading, no trait/interface dispatch, no `match` exhaustiveness
// checking (see `Checker.hotc`'s own header), no `module` declarations. The entry-point
// `main(String[])` wrapper `compile_program` builds still assumes the designated entry fn takes
// `Int` args and returns `Int` -- true of every demo program this phase, not touched since none of
// them need it lifted.

extern class JObject4 = "java.lang.Object" {}
extern class Label = "org.objectweb.asm.Label" {
    fn new() -> Self;
}
// `@"binary.Name"(...)` -- see `Ast.hc`'s own `Annotation` header. `visit`/`visitEnum` take a
// non-null `name` (this is the top-level, NAMED-argument shape); an array ELEMENT is unnamed by
// definition, which is why those go through `CodegenShim`'s own `visitArrayString`/`visitArrayEnum`
// instead (see that file's own header on the real Java-`null`-argument gap).
extern class AnnotationVisitor2 = "org.objectweb.asm.AnnotationVisitor" {
    fn visit(&mut self, name: String, value: JObject4);
    fn visitEnum(&mut self, name: String, descriptor: String, value: String);
    fn visitArray(&mut self, name: String) -> AnnotationVisitor2;
    fn visitEnd(&mut self);
}
extern class MethodVisitor2 = "org.objectweb.asm.MethodVisitor" {
    fn visitCode(&mut self);
    fn visitVarInsn(&mut self, opcode: Int, varIndex: Int);
    fn visitIntInsn(&mut self, opcode: Int, operand: Int);
    fn visitInsn(&mut self, opcode: Int);
    fn visitJumpInsn(&mut self, opcode: Int, label: &Label);
    fn visitLabel(&mut self, label: &Label);
    fn visitLdcInsn(&mut self, value: JObject4);
    fn visitTypeInsn(&mut self, opcode: Int, type: String);
    fn visitMethodInsn(&mut self, opcode: Int, owner: String, name: String, descriptor: String, isInterface: Bool);
    fn visitFieldInsn(&mut self, opcode: Int, owner: String, name: String, descriptor: String);
    fn visitMaxs(&mut self, maxStack: Int, maxLocals: Int);
    fn visitEnd(&mut self);
    // `type` is the exception's real JVM-internal (slash-form) binary name -- real `try`/`catch`
    // support, added for classpath reflection's own `ClassNotFoundException` handling (see
    // `Ast.hc`'s own `Try` header).
    fn visitTryCatchBlock(&mut self, start: &Label, end: &Label, handler: &Label, type: String);
    // Real per-statement debug info -- ties a bytecode offset (`start`, a fresh `Label` marked
    // right before it) back to a source line, the piece a JVM debugger needs for breakpoints/
    // stepping (and a stack trace needs for a real line number instead of just a method name).
    fn visitLineNumber(&mut self, line: Int, start: &Label);
    // See `ClassWriter2`'s own matching header -- always `true` (RUNTIME retention) at every real
    // call site, never a source-level knob.
    fn visitAnnotation(&mut self, descriptor: String, visible: Bool) -> AnnotationVisitor2;
}
extern class ClassWriter2 = "org.objectweb.asm.ClassWriter" {
    fn new(flags: Int) -> Self;
    fn visit(&mut self, version: Int, access: Int, name: String, signature: String, superName: String, interfaces: [String]);
    fn visitMethod(&mut self, access: Int, name: String, descriptor: String, signature: String, exceptions: [String]) -> MethodVisitor2;
    fn visitEnd(&mut self);
    // `@"binary.Name"(...)` on a top-level `struct` -- see `Ast.hc`'s own `Annotation` header.
    // `visible` is always `true` (`RuntimeVisibleAnnotations`, not `RuntimeInvisibleAnnotations`)
    // at every real call site here -- reflection-driven consumers need it at runtime, and there's
    // no source-level knob to ask for less.
    fn visitAnnotation(&mut self, descriptor: String, visible: Bool) -> AnnotationVisitor2;
}
extern class CodegenShim = "hc.selfhost.codegen.CodegenShim" {
    fn writeClass(cw: ClassWriter2, path: String);
    // See CodegenShim.java's own comment: HC's `null` literal can't be passed as a bare call
    // argument (only against an already-known nullable expected type), so building a non-static
    // field's `visitField` call (whose last param must be a real Java `null`) stays on the Java
    // side entirely.
    fn visitInstanceField(cw: &mut ClassWriter2, access: Int, name: String, descriptor: String);
    fn visitClassHeader(cw: &mut ClassWriter2, version: Int, access: Int, name: String, superName: String, interfaces: [String]);
    fn visitMethodNoSig(cw: &mut ClassWriter2, access: Int, name: String, descriptor: String, exceptions: [String]) -> MethodVisitor2;
    // `ClassWriter.visitSource(source, null)` -- see this file's own header on why the `null`
    // stays on the Java side. Gives every class this compiler emits a real `.hotc` source file
    // name in its `SourceFile` attribute (stack traces, and a JVM debugger's own source lookup).
    fn visitSource(cw: &mut ClassWriter2, source: String);
    // Same real `null`-`name` gap as `AnnotationVisitor2`'s own header describes -- an unnamed
    // annotation ARRAY element.
    fn visitArrayString(av: &mut AnnotationVisitor2, value: String);
    fn visitArrayEnum(av: &mut AnnotationVisitor2, descriptor: String, value: String);
}

// Whole-program metadata `gen_fn` needs beyond the one `FnDecl` it's currently emitting --
// bundled into one struct (rather than several separate `Vec` params) purely to keep `gen_fn`'s
// own signature from growing a new parameter every time codegen learns about one more kind of
// cross-function/cross-struct lookup.
pub struct ProgramInfo {
    // The JDK target this compile is for (17/21/25) -- see `CodeGen.target`'s own header.
    // Carried through `ProgramInfo` (rather than a separate parameter on every fn that already
    // takes `info: ProgramInfo`) purely so `build_codegen` can read `info.target` directly when
    // constructing a fresh `CodeGen` per function/method, with zero new parameters threaded
    // through `gen_fn`/`gen_method`/`gen_static_init` themselves.
    target: Int,
    fn_names: Vec<String>,
    fn_descs_list: Vec<String>,
    fn_rettys_list: Vec<String>,
    // See this list's own construction site in `build_program_info` for why it exists.
    fn_param_types_list: Vec<Vec<String>>,
    // Parallel to `fn_param_types_list` (same index, same fn) -- the DECLARED param NAMES, in
    // order, for named-argument resolution (`gen_expr`'s own `Call` arm, `resolve_named_args_cg`
    // -- see README's own "Named arguments" section).
    fn_param_names_list: Vec<Vec<String>>,
    // A generic top-level fn's own name -> the index (into its `params`) of the ONE param whose
    // declared type is exactly the bare type parameter (`fn identity<T>(x: T)` -- index 0) --
    // `gen_expr`'s own `Call` codegen reads this to infer a concrete type argument at each call
    // site (see `Checker.hotc`'s own `check_generic_call`, redone independently here since this
    // file doesn't share the checker's own registries).
    // Top-level `pub static NAME: Type = expr;` name -> declared type name -- `gen_expr`'s own
    // `Ident` codegen checks this (after checking it's not a known LOCAL first, so a local can
    // still shadow a static of the same name) to decide `GETSTATIC` vs `ILOAD`/`ALOAD`.
    static_names: Vec<String>,
    static_type_names: Vec<String>,
    generic_fn_names: Vec<String>,
    generic_fn_infer_idx: Vec<Int>,
    // Parallel to `generic_fn_names`/`generic_fn_infer_idx` -- the SECOND type param's own
    // inference index (`-1` when the fn has only one type param), see the `CodeGen` struct's own
    // `generic_fn_infer_idx2` header.
    generic_fn_infer_idx2: Vec<Int>,
    generic_fn_infer_unwrap2: Vec<Int>,
    // Parallel to `generic_fn_infer_idx` (1 = unwrap, 0 = use directly): does the chosen param's
    // OWN declared type wrap the bare type param inside a generic struct/enum (`b: Box<T>`, parsed
    // to the mono-shaped string `"Box$T"` by `type_name_ref` -- see `Ast.hc`'s own header on why
    // `Base<Arg>` types are strings, not a separate AST shape) rather than being the bare type
    // param directly (`x: T`)? A real, reported gap: `fn unwrap<T>(b: Box<T>) -> T` called as
    // `unwrap(boxInt)` used to fail with "could not infer generic type argument" -- the arg's own
    // codegen-time type at the call site is `"Box$Int"` (a real mono struct name), not `"Int"`
    // directly, so the inferred type argument needs one `mono_arg(...)` unwrap first. Mirrors
    // `Checker.hotc`'s own `check_generic_call` fix for the identical gap -- redone independently
    // here since this file doesn't share the checker's own registries (same split every other
    // "generic_fn_*" field in this struct already has).
    generic_fn_infer_unwrap: Vec<Int>,
    // A generic struct's own bare name ("Box") -> the name of the ONE field whose declared type is
    // exactly the bare type parameter ("value", for `struct Box<T> { value: T }`) -- lets
    // `gen_expr`'s own `StructLit` codegen infer a concrete instantiation from a field LITERAL's
    // own shape (`shape_only_ty_cg`) when no other context (return-position `self.ret_ty`, the
    // "Vec { data: ... }" idiom) resolves it. Mirrors `Checker.hotc`'s own `check_struct_lit` fix
    // for the identical gap (`let boxInt = Box { value: 42 };`, no declared-type annotation to
    // infer from at this file's own -- unlike the checker's `generic_templates` registry, this file
    // has no equivalent, so this pair of parallel Vecs is that lookup's whole substitute).
    generic_struct_infer_names: Vec<String>,
    generic_struct_infer_fields: Vec<String>,
    // Enum-variant sibling of the pair above -- keyed by VARIANT name ("Some"), not the enum's own
    // base name ("Option"), since which field carries the inference is a per-variant question.
    generic_enum_infer_variant_names: Vec<String>,
    generic_enum_infer_owner_names: Vec<String>,
    generic_enum_infer_field_names: Vec<String>,
    // Every real enum's own name (plain AND generic mono instantiations alike) -- `struct_names`/
    // `struct_field_*_list` don't distinguish "real struct" from "enum, field-lookup purposes" at
    // all (same simplification `Checker.hotc`'s own `structs` registry already makes), but
    // `gen_binary`'s own `==`/`!=` codegen needs to tell the two apart for real (an enum's `tag`
    // field never gets recorded in `struct_field_*_list` at all -- it's a pure codegen-time detail
    // added directly in `gen_enum`, so checking for "tag" among a type's registered fields can
    // never work; this is the real signal instead). See `CodeGen`'s own `is_enum_type`.
    enum_names: Vec<String>,
    struct_names: Vec<String>,
    struct_field_names_list: Vec<Vec<String>>,
    struct_field_types_list: Vec<Vec<String>>,
    // Keyed by "StructName#methodName" -- same flat-key simplification `Checker.hotc`'s own
    // `methods` field makes.
    method_keys: Vec<String>,
    method_descs_list: Vec<String>,
    method_rettys_list: Vec<String>,
    // Parallel to `method_keys` (same index, same method) -- the DECLARED param type NAMES, in
    // order. Real gap found building Phoenix Flight's own `pool.spawn(|| ...)`: a lambda literal
    // passed to a PLAIN (non-extern) struct method's own `&dyn Interface` param had no way to
    // learn that param's target type at codegen time -- `struct_methods`'s own JVM descriptor
    // string bakes the interface's BINARY name in, not recoverable back into a per-position HC
    // type name `gen_lambda` can use. See `finish_method_call`'s own header for the fix this
    // feeds.
    method_param_types_list: Vec<Vec<String>>,
    // Variant name -> owning enum's own name (parallel arrays, same "name" -- "owner" shape as
    // `Checker.hotc`'s `variant_owner`).
    variant_owner_names: Vec<String>,
    variant_owner_enums: Vec<String>,
    // Keyed by "EnumName#VariantName" -> that variant's tag index (declaration order).
    variant_tag_keys: Vec<String>,
    variant_tag_list: Vec<Int>,
    // extern class alias name -> its real binary (internal-slash) name.
    extern_alias_names: Vec<String>,
    extern_binary_names: Vec<String>,
    // Parallel to `extern_alias_names`/`extern_binary_names` -- 1 when that alias's own
    // `ExternClassDecl.is_interface` was set (see `Ast.hc`'s own header), 0 otherwise (including
    // every entry folded in from an UNRELATED `extern interface` declaration, which always
    // dispatches through the separate `dyn`/`INVOKEINTERFACE` mechanism already, never this
    // registry). `gen_expr`'s `MethodCall` codegen reads this to choose `INVOKEINTERFACE` over
    // the usual `INVOKEVIRTUAL` for an extern-class instance call.
    extern_alias_is_interface: Vec<Int>,
    // Keyed by "Alias#methodName" -- covers constructors, static methods, and instance methods
    // alike (same flat-key convention `method_keys` above uses for struct methods).
    extern_method_keys: Vec<String>,
    extern_method_descs_list: Vec<String>,
    extern_method_rettys_list: Vec<String>,
    extern_method_static_list: Vec<Int>,
    extern_method_ctor_list: Vec<Int>,
    // See this list's own construction site in `build_program_info` for why it exists: the
    // DECLARED param type name strings, not yet compiled to JVM descriptors.
    extern_method_param_types_list: Vec<Vec<String>>,
    // Keyed by "Alias#fieldName" -- a real field on the underlying Java class (see `Ast.hc`'s own
    // `ExternField` header). `_descs_list` is the field's real JVM descriptor (for GETFIELD/PUTFIELD/
    // GETSTATIC/PUTSTATIC), `_type_names_list` its raw HC type name (`gen_expr`'s error-free lookups
    // don't need a descriptor at all), `_static_list` the usual 0/1 flag.
    extern_field_keys: Vec<String>,
    extern_field_descs_list: Vec<String>,
    extern_field_type_names_list: Vec<String>,
    extern_field_static_list: Vec<Int>,
    // struct name -> the extern-class alias it `extends`, present only for a real `extends`
    // struct (see `Ast.hc`'s own `StructDecl.extends` header). `gen_expr`'s `StaticCall` fallback
    // (an extends-struct's own `::new(...)`) is the only reader.
    struct_extends_names: Vec<String>,
    struct_extends_targets: Vec<String>,
    // Keyed by "EnumName#VariantName" -- that VARIANT's own field table alone, not the enum-wide
    // union `struct_field_*_list` registers it under (see `field_type_of`'s own header/`Match`
    // codegen's use of it). Real gap found self-compiling `Checker.hotc`: `Ast.hc`'s own `Expr`
    // enum reuses the field name "value" across many variants at different types, so
    // `field_type_of`'s linear scan over the UNIONED table always resolved a match arm's "value"
    // binding to whichever variant registered first (`IntLit`'s `Int`), corrupting every OTHER
    // variant's own field slots/descriptors -- mirrors `Checker.hotc`'s own `variant_field_info`
    // fix for the exact same root cause, fixed independently here (a separate pass, not fed
    // anything back from the checker's own run).
    variant_field_keys: Vec<String>,
    variant_field_names_list: Vec<Vec<String>>,
    variant_field_types_list: Vec<Vec<String>>,
    // A struct/enum/interface's own name (its bare template name for generics -- see
    // `mono_base_cg`) -> the dot-joined `module a.b.c;` string it was declared under, if any.
    // Only names that actually had a `module` declaration in scope appear here; everything else
    // (default package, extern interfaces -- their `extern_binary_name` is already the real final
    // name) is absent, and `qualify_names_cg`/`CodeGen.qualify` both treat "absent" as "leave the
    // bare name alone, default package" -- the same backward-compatible fallback every other
    // lookup registry in this file already uses.
    module_names: Vec<String>,
    module_values: Vec<String>,
    // The full, raw interface declaration list (local AND extern alike) -- `gen_expr`'s own
    // `gen_lambda` needs this to resolve a lambda literal's target interface (real JVM binary
    // name via `struct_iface_jvm_name`, its one abstract method's real name/params/return type),
    // and nothing already threaded into `CodeGen` covers that (every other interface-shaped
    // registry here only tracks what a STRUCT implements, not an interface's own method table).
    interfaces_raw: Vec<InterfaceDecl>,
    // `@sendable struct Foo { ... }` -- see `Ast.hc`'s own `Program.sendable_struct_names` header.
    // `build_codegen` populates `CodeGen.sendable_structs` from this directly; the ONLY reader is
    // Phoenix Flight's own `PhoenixPool::spawn` capture check in `finish_method_call`.
    sendable_struct_names: Vec<String>,
}

pub struct CodeGen {
    mv: MethodVisitor2,
    // `slots`/`var_types` are only the CURRENT (innermost) lexical scope's own local-variable
    // bookkeeping -- `slot_scopes`/`var_type_scopes` hold every enclosing scope, mirroring
    // `Checker.hotc`'s own `TyChecker.var_scopes` (a separate, independent copy -- this file keeps
    // its own local-variable state throughout, same "checker and codegen don't share registries"
    // split as everywhere else). `push_var_scope`/`pop_var_scope`/`lookup_type`/`lookup_slot` are
    // the real read/scope-management path; `gen_block` (every `{ ... }`) pushes/pops one around
    // itself, same as `Checker.hotc`'s own `check_block`. `next_slot` is NOT part of this scoping --
    // JVM local slots are never reused across sibling blocks here, so it only ever grows.
    slots: Registry<Int>,
    slot_scopes: Vec<Registry<Int>>,
    next_slot: Int,
    owner: String,
    // The JDK target this whole compile is for (17/21/25) -- see the compiler's own `--target`
    // CLI flag (`Driver.hotc`'s own `run_cli`). Copied straight through from `info.target`
    // (`build_codegen`'s own literal) or `self.target` (`clone_codegen_for_lambda`'s), same
    // "passed through unchanged" pattern `owner` right above already uses. `gen_lambda`'s own
    // synthesized-class header is the only reader on THIS struct -- every OTHER class-header
    // emission site (`gen_struct`/`gen_enum`/`gen_interface`/`compile_program`'s own entry
    // class) reads a plain `target: Int` PARAMETER instead, since those are free functions with
    // no `self` to carry it on.
    target: Int,
    fn_descs: Registry<String>,
    fn_ret_tys: Registry<String>,
    // See `ProgramInfo.fn_param_types_list`'s own header.
    fn_param_types: Registry<Vec<String>>,
    // See `ProgramInfo.fn_param_names_list`'s own header.
    fn_param_names: Registry<Vec<String>>,
    var_types: Registry<String>,
    var_type_scopes: Vec<Registry<String>>,
    ret_ty: String,
    struct_fields: Registry<Vec<String>>,
    struct_ftypes: Registry<Vec<String>>,
    struct_methods: Registry<String>,
    struct_method_rettys: Registry<String>,
    // See `ProgramInfo.method_param_types_list`'s own header.
    struct_method_param_types: Registry<Vec<String>>,
    // See `ProgramInfo.sendable_struct_names`'s own header.
    sendable_structs: Registry<Bool>,
    variant_owner: Registry<String>,
    variant_tags: Registry<Int>,
    extern_binary: Registry<String>,
    extern_is_interface: Registry<Int>,
    extern_method_descs: Registry<String>,
    extern_method_rettys: Registry<String>,
    extern_method_static: Registry<Int>,
    extern_method_ctor: Registry<Int>,
    // See `ProgramInfo.extern_method_param_types_list`'s own header.
    extern_method_param_types: Registry<Vec<String>>,
    // See `ProgramInfo.extern_field_keys`'s own header.
    extern_field_descs: Registry<String>,
    extern_field_type_names: Registry<String>,
    extern_field_static: Registry<Int>,
    // See `ProgramInfo.struct_extends_names`'s own header.
    struct_extends: Registry<String>,
    generic_fn_infer_idx: Registry<Int>,
    generic_fn_infer_unwrap: Registry<Int>,
    // `fn hash_map2_new<K: Hashable, V>(...)` -- the SECOND type param's own inference metadata,
    // mirroring `generic_fn_infer_idx`/`_unwrap` exactly (see `Ast.hc`'s own `FnDecl.type_param2`
    // header). A generic fn with only one type param gets `-1`/`0` here (see `build_program_info`'s
    // own population loop) -- `gen_expr`'s own `Call` codegen only appends a second `"$..."`
    // segment to the mono target name when this is present.
    generic_fn_infer_idx2: Registry<Int>,
    generic_fn_infer_unwrap2: Registry<Int>,
    generic_struct_infer_field: Registry<String>,
    generic_enum_infer_owner: Registry<String>,
    generic_enum_infer_field: Registry<String>,
    static_types: Registry<String>,
    enum_names: Registry<Bool>,
    // The innermost enclosing `while`'s own start/end labels, for `Break`/`Continue` codegen (a
    // plain GOTO to one or the other -- no drop-list to emit first, this subset has no `Drop`/RAII
    // at all). Saved into locals and restored around a nested `While`'s own body codegen (mirroring
    // the checker's `loop_depth` save/restore) rather than a real label STACK, since codegen only
    // ever needs the CURRENT innermost loop's labels, never an outer one mid-nested-loop.
    cur_loop_start: Label,
    cur_loop_end: Label,
    // Keyed "EnumName#VariantName" -- see `ProgramInfo`'s own matching field header.
    variant_field_names: Registry<Vec<String>>,
    variant_field_types: Registry<Vec<String>>,
    // The most recent source line `mark_line` actually emitted a `LineNumberTable` entry for --
    // `-1` (never equals a real 1-based line number) at the start of a method, so the FIRST
    // `mark_line` call always emits, same real convention the real compiler's own `lastEmittedLine`
    // uses. See `mark_line`'s own header.
    last_emitted_line: Int,
    // See `ProgramInfo.module_names`'s own header -- same registry, built from the same source,
    // just wrapped as a `Registry<String>` like `extern_binary` is, for O(1)-ish lookup from
    // methods (rather than the linear scan the free-function codegen sites use via
    // `qualify_names_cg`/`find_struct_module_cg`/`find_enum_module_cg`, which don't have a `&mut
    // self` to hang a `Registry` off of).
    module_of: Registry<String>,
    // See `ProgramInfo.interfaces_raw`'s own header -- carried through unchanged, `gen_lambda`'s
    // one real consumer.
    interfaces_raw: Vec<InterfaceDecl>,
    // A short, JVM-identifier-safe string naming whichever fn/method THIS `CodeGen` instance is
    // currently compiling the body of (`"main"` for a bare top-level fn, `"Struct_method"` for an
    // instance/static method -- see `gen_fn`/`gen_method`'s own call sites) -- `gen_lambda`'s own
    // synthesized class name (`self.owner + "$" + fn_identity + "$Lambda" + lambda_counter`) needs
    // this to stay globally unique across the WHOLE compiled program: `build_codegen` itself is
    // called fresh per fn/method body (a new `CodeGen`, `lambda_counter` reset to 0 each time), so
    // `lambda_counter` alone only disambiguates MULTIPLE lambdas within the SAME fn/method, not
    // across two different ones that each happen to have their own "first" lambda.
    fn_identity: String,
    lambda_counter: Int,
}

impl CodeGen {
    // `Long`/`Double` are real JVM category-2 primitives -- each occupies TWO consecutive local
    // slots, not one, same as every other JVM-targeting compiler has to account for (the JVM spec
    // itself requires this; it's not a convention this compiler invented). `Float` is category-1,
    // same as `Int` -- only `Double` needs the same treatment `Long` already gets. Every other type
    // this subset knows about (`Int`/`Bool`/`Float`/`String`/struct/enum references) is category-1,
    // one slot.
    fn alloc_slot(&mut self, name: String, ty: String) -> Int {
        let slot = self.next_slot;
        self.slots.register(name, slot);
        if ty == "Long" || ty == "Double" {
            self.next_slot = slot + 2;
        } else {
            self.next_slot = slot + 1;
        }
        return slot;
    }

    fn slot_of(&mut self, name: String) -> Int {
        let found = self.lookup_slot(name);
        if let Some { value: s } = found {
            return s;
        } else {
            return 0;
        }
    }

    fn type_of(&mut self, name: String) -> String {
        let found = self.lookup_type(name);
        if let Some { value: t } = found {
            return t;
        } else {
            return "Int";
        }
    }

    // Enter a new lexical scope for LOCAL VARIABLE bookkeeping only (`var_types`/`slots`) -- see
    // `Checker.hotc`'s own `push_scope`/`TyChecker.var_scopes` header for the full design this
    // mirrors. `next_slot` is deliberately NOT reset here: JVM local slots are never reused across
    // sibling blocks in this compiler (every `alloc_slot` call just keeps growing `next_slot`), so
    // popping only needs to restore which NAME resolves to which already-allocated slot/type, not
    // reclaim the slot number itself.
    fn push_var_scope(&mut self) {
        self.var_type_scopes.push(self.var_types);
        self.slot_scopes.push(self.slots);
        self.var_types = registry_new("__init__", "Int");
        self.slots = registry_new("__init__", -1);
    }

    fn pop_var_scope(&mut self) {
        self.var_types = self.var_type_scopes.pop();
        self.slots = self.slot_scopes.pop();
    }

    // The one true read path for a local's declared type -- innermost scope first, then each
    // enclosing one -- mirrors `Checker.hotc`'s own `lookup_var` exactly (this file keeps its own,
    // independent copy of local-variable bookkeeping, same "checker and codegen don't share
    // registries" split as everywhere else in this compiler).
    fn lookup_type(&mut self, name: String) -> Option<String> {
        let found = self.var_types.get(name);
        if let Some { value: t } = found {
            return Option<String>::Some { value: t };
        }
        var i = self.var_type_scopes.length() - 1;
        while i >= 0 {
            let scope = self.var_type_scopes.get(i);
            let f2 = scope.get(name);
            if let Some { value: t2 } = f2 {
                return Option<String>::Some { value: t2 };
            }
            i = i - 1;
        }
        return Option<String>::None {};
    }

    // Same as `lookup_type`, for the matching JVM local slot number.
    fn lookup_slot(&mut self, name: String) -> Option<Int> {
        let found = self.slots.get(name);
        if let Some { value: s } = found {
            return Option<Int>::Some { value: s };
        }
        var i = self.slot_scopes.length() - 1;
        while i >= 0 {
            let scope = self.slot_scopes.get(i);
            let f2 = scope.get(name);
            if let Some { value: s2 } = f2 {
                return Option<Int>::Some { value: s2 };
            }
            i = i - 1;
        }
        return Option<Int>::None {};
    }

    // Marks a real `LineNumberTable` entry (a fresh `Label` right at the current bytecode offset,
    // tied back to `line`) once per DISTINCT line, right before that statement's own bytecode --
    // mirrors the real compiler's own `markLine` exactly. Skipping a repeat of the same line (two
    // statements sharing one, e.g. after formatting collapses them) avoids two redundant entries
    // for the identical line; `line <= 0` (the `0` placeholder every synthetic/generated statement
    // this codebase's own `empty_stmt_vec`-style seeds and generated-source entry stubs use) is
    // skipped too -- there's no real source line to report for code that was never actually parsed
    // from a `.hotc` file at all.
    fn mark_line(&mut self, line: Int) {
        if line <= 0 { return; }
        if line == self.last_emitted_line { return; }
        self.last_emitted_line = line;
        var label = Label::new();
        self.mv.visitLabel(&label);
        self.mv.visitLineNumber(line, &label);
    }

    fn field_type_of(&mut self, struct_name: String, field: String) -> String {
        let names_found = self.struct_fields.get(struct_name);
        let types_found = self.struct_ftypes.get(struct_name);
        if let Some { value: names } = names_found {
            if let Some { value: types } = types_found {
                var i = 0;
                while i < names.length() {
                    if names.get(i) == field {
                        return types.get(i);
                    }
                    i = i + 1;
                }
            }
        }
        throw CodegenError::new("codegen: unknown field '" + field + "' on struct '" + struct_name + "'");
    }

    // Sequential packing, no alignment padding -- narrow fields (`Int`/`Bool`) 4 bytes, `Long` 8 --
    // exactly mirroring the real compiler's own `ArenaLayout` (see that class's own header, ported
    // as-is: it makes the identical simplification, not a new scope cut this port invented).
    fn arena_elem_size(&mut self, struct_name: String) -> Int {
        let names_found = self.struct_fields.get(struct_name);
        let types_found = self.struct_ftypes.get(struct_name);
        var size = 0;
        if let Some { value: names } = names_found {
            if let Some { value: types } = types_found {
                var i = 0;
                while i < types.length() {
                    if types.get(i) == "Long" { size = size + 8; } else { size = size + 4; }
                    i = i + 1;
                }
            }
        }
        return size;
    }

    fn arena_field_offset(&mut self, struct_name: String, field: String) -> Int {
        let names_found = self.struct_fields.get(struct_name);
        let types_found = self.struct_ftypes.get(struct_name);
        var offset = 0;
        if let Some { value: names } = names_found {
            if let Some { value: types } = types_found {
                var i = 0;
                while i < names.length() {
                    if names.get(i) == field { return offset; }
                    if types.get(i) == "Long" { offset = offset + 8; } else { offset = offset + 4; }
                    i = i + 1;
                }
            }
        }
        return -1;
    }

    // Same `SIPUSH`-with-a-real-`LDC`-fallback convention `IntLit`'s own codegen already
    // established (see that arm's own header) -- an arena struct's element size/field offset are
    // ordinary compile-time-known `Int` constants, not immune to the same `SIPUSH` 16-bit range
    // limit a large-enough struct could exceed.
    fn push_small_int(&mut self, value: Int) {
        if value >= -32768 && value <= 32767 {
            self.mv.visitIntInsn(17, value); // SIPUSH
        } else {
            let boxed = JIntegerCg::valueOf(value);
            self.mv.visitLdcInsn(boxed as JObject4);
            self.mv.visitMethodInsn(182, "java/lang/Integer", "intValue", "()I", false); // unbox back to a real int
        }
    }

    // `Int`/`Bool` fields map to `ValueLayout.JAVA_INT`, `Long` to `JAVA_LONG` -- anything else
    // (`Float`/`Double`/a struct-typed field) falls back to `JAVA_INT` too, exactly matching the
    // real compiler's own `valueLayoutField`'s identical fallback (never really supported there
    // either -- see `Ast.hc`'s own `StructDecl.is_arena` header).
    fn arena_layout_field_name(&mut self, fty: String) -> String {
        if fty == "Bool" { return "JAVA_BOOLEAN"; }
        if fty == "Long" { return "JAVA_LONG"; }
        return "JAVA_INT";
    }

    fn arena_layout_desc(&mut self, fty: String) -> String {
        if fty == "Bool" { return "Ljava/lang/foreign/ValueLayout$OfBoolean;"; }
        if fty == "Long" { return "Ljava/lang/foreign/ValueLayout$OfLong;"; }
        return "Ljava/lang/foreign/ValueLayout$OfInt;";
    }

    fn arena_prim_desc(&mut self, fty: String) -> String {
        if fty == "Bool" { return "Z"; }
        if fty == "Long" { return "J"; }
        return "I";
    }

    // `arena Name[count]` -- `Arena.ofAuto()` (a GC-managed lifetime -- the buffer stays alive as
    // long as it's reachable, no explicit `close()`/try-with-resources needed) then
    // `Arena.allocate(byteSize, byteAlignment)`, both real `java.lang.foreign` interface methods
    // (note `isInterface = true` on BOTH invocations below, including the static `ofAuto()` call --
    // a real ASM requirement for a static method declared on an interface, not a typo). Mirrors the
    // real compiler's own `genArenaNew` exactly.
    fn gen_arena_new(&mut self, struct_name: String, count: &Expr) -> String {
        let elem_size = self.arena_elem_size(struct_name);
        self.mv.visitMethodInsn(184, "java/lang/foreign/Arena", "ofAuto", "()Ljava/lang/foreign/Arena;", true); // INVOKESTATIC
        self.gen_expr(count);
        self.push_small_int(elem_size);
        self.mv.visitInsn(104); // IMUL -- count * elemSize
        self.mv.visitInsn(133); // I2L
        self.push_small_int(4);
        self.mv.visitInsn(133); // I2L -- byteAlignment
        self.mv.visitMethodInsn(185, "java/lang/foreign/Arena", "allocate", "(JJ)Ljava/lang/foreign/MemorySegment;", true); // INVOKEINTERFACE
        return "arena " + struct_name;
    }

    // `"a{x}b"` -- a real `StringBuilder` chain (`NEW`/`DUP`/`<init>`, then one `.append(...)` call
    // per literal segment and per interpolated value, finished with `.toString()`), the exact
    // per-type `append` overload picked by the interpolated expression's OWN runtime type -- same
    // "match the real Java overload by hand" convention `print`'s own codegen already established.
    // Mirrors the real compiler's own `genStringInterp` exactly. An empty literal segment (two
    // interpolations back to back, `"{x}{y}"`) skips its own `append` call entirely -- appending
    // `""` would be correct but pointlessly bulks up the bytecode for no observable difference.
    fn gen_string_interp(&mut self, literals: Vec<String>, exprs: Vec<Expr>) -> String {
        self.mv.visitTypeInsn(187, "java/lang/StringBuilder"); // NEW
        self.mv.visitInsn(89); // DUP
        self.mv.visitMethodInsn(183, "java/lang/StringBuilder", "<init>", "()V", false); // INVOKESPECIAL
        let first = literals.get(0);
        if first.length() > 0 {
            self.mv.visitLdcInsn(first as JObject4);
            self.mv.visitMethodInsn(182, "java/lang/StringBuilder", "append", "(Ljava/lang/String;)Ljava/lang/StringBuilder;", false);
        }
        var i = 0;
        while i < exprs.length() {
            let ety = self.gen_expr(exprs.get(i));
            var desc = "(Ljava/lang/String;)Ljava/lang/StringBuilder;";
            if ety == "Int" { desc = "(I)Ljava/lang/StringBuilder;"; }
            if ety == "Long" { desc = "(J)Ljava/lang/StringBuilder;"; }
            if ety == "Float" { desc = "(F)Ljava/lang/StringBuilder;"; }
            if ety == "Double" { desc = "(D)Ljava/lang/StringBuilder;"; }
            if ety == "Bool" { desc = "(Z)Ljava/lang/StringBuilder;"; }
            if ety == "Char" { desc = "(C)Ljava/lang/StringBuilder;"; }
            // `StringBuilder` has no real `append(byte)` overload -- same auto-widen-to-`int`
            // reasoning `print`'s own descriptor picker header already explains.
            if ety == "Byte" { desc = "(I)Ljava/lang/StringBuilder;"; }
            self.mv.visitMethodInsn(182, "java/lang/StringBuilder", "append", desc, false);
            let lit = literals.get(i + 1);
            if lit.length() > 0 {
                self.mv.visitLdcInsn(lit as JObject4);
                self.mv.visitMethodInsn(182, "java/lang/StringBuilder", "append", "(Ljava/lang/String;)Ljava/lang/StringBuilder;", false);
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        self.mv.visitMethodInsn(182, "java/lang/StringBuilder", "toString", "()Ljava/lang/String;", false);
        return "String";
    }

    // `(index * elemSize + fieldOffset) as Long` -- the byte offset `MemorySegment.get`/`.set`
    // both take as their own second argument. Mirrors the real compiler's own `pushArenaOffset`.
    fn gen_arena_offset(&mut self, index: &Expr, struct_name: String, field: String) {
        let elem_size = self.arena_elem_size(struct_name);
        let field_offset = self.arena_field_offset(struct_name, field);
        self.gen_expr(index);
        self.push_small_int(elem_size);
        self.mv.visitInsn(104); // IMUL
        self.push_small_int(field_offset);
        self.mv.visitInsn(96); // IADD
        self.mv.visitInsn(133); // I2L
    }

    // `buf[i].field` (a read) -- `arr_name` is the plain local/param identifier holding the
    // `MemorySegment` (see `Ast.hc`'s own `StructDecl.is_arena` header for why only that shape is
    // supported this phase, not an arbitrary expression).
    fn gen_arena_field_get(&mut self, arr_name: String, index: &Expr, struct_name: String, field: String) -> String {
        let fty = self.field_type_of(struct_name, field);
        let layout_field = self.arena_layout_field_name(fty);
        let layout_desc = self.arena_layout_desc(fty);
        let prim_desc = self.arena_prim_desc(fty);
        self.gen_expr(Ident { name: arr_name }); // pushes the MemorySegment
        self.mv.visitFieldInsn(178, "java/lang/foreign/ValueLayout", layout_field, layout_desc); // GETSTATIC
        self.gen_arena_offset(index, struct_name, field);
        self.mv.visitMethodInsn(185, "java/lang/foreign/MemorySegment", "get", "(" + layout_desc + "J)" + prim_desc, true); // INVOKEINTERFACE
        return fty;
    }

    // `buf[i].field = value` (a write).
    // `MemorySegment.set(...)` returns `void` (unlike a real `PUTFIELD`, whose own operand-stack
    // shape a `DUP_X1` can cheaply preserve a copy across -- see the ordinary, non-arena
    // `FieldAssign` arm's own comment), so there's nothing on the stack afterward to serve as this
    // assignment EXPRESSION's own result value (needed if it's ever used as a sub-expression, not
    // just a bare statement, matching every other assignment form's semantics). An unregistered
    // temp local slot sidesteps the problem entirely: evaluate `value` once into it, reload it for
    // the actual `.set()` call, then reload it again afterward as this expression's own value --
    // same "raw, unregistered slot" trick already established elsewhere in this file (`match`'s own
    // subject, `ArrayRepeat`'s own fill value).
    fn gen_arena_field_set(&mut self, arr_name: String, index: &Expr, struct_name: String, field: String, value: &Expr) -> String {
        let fty = self.field_type_of(struct_name, field);
        let layout_field = self.arena_layout_field_name(fty);
        let layout_desc = self.arena_layout_desc(fty);
        let prim_desc = self.arena_prim_desc(fty);
        let vty = self.gen_expr(value);
        let tmp_slot = self.next_slot;
        if fty == "Long" { self.next_slot = self.next_slot + 2; } else { self.next_slot = self.next_slot + 1; }
        self.mv.visitVarInsn(store_opcode(fty), tmp_slot);
        self.gen_expr(Ident { name: arr_name }); // pushes the MemorySegment
        self.mv.visitFieldInsn(178, "java/lang/foreign/ValueLayout", layout_field, layout_desc); // GETSTATIC
        self.gen_arena_offset(index, struct_name, field);
        self.mv.visitVarInsn(load_opcode(fty), tmp_slot);
        self.mv.visitMethodInsn(185, "java/lang/foreign/MemorySegment", "set", "(" + layout_desc + "J" + prim_desc + ")V", true); // INVOKEINTERFACE
        self.mv.visitVarInsn(load_opcode(fty), tmp_slot); // leave a copy as this expression's own result
        return vty;
    }

    // The ordinary (non-arena) tail of `FieldAccess` codegen -- `.length` on an array, or a real
    // `GETFIELD` on a struct -- factored out so both the plain-`obj` case and the "`obj` turned out
    // to be a non-arena `Index`" fallback in `FieldAccess`'s own arm can share it, rather than
    // duplicating the array-vs-struct branch twice.
    fn finish_field_access(&mut self, ot: String, field: String) -> String {
        if is_array_type_name_cg(ot) {
            if field != "length" {
                throw CodegenError::new("array has no field '" + field + "' (only 'length')");
            }
            self.mv.visitInsn(190); // ARRAYLENGTH
            return "Int";
        }
        let ebinary_found0 = self.extern_binary.get(ot);
        if let Some { value: ebinary0 } = ebinary_found0 {
            // `ot` names a known `extern class` alias, not a struct -- an instance field on the
            // real underlying Java class (see `Ast.hc`'s own `ExternField` header), not a struct
            // field, so `field_type_of`/`self.qualify` (both struct-only) don't apply here.
            let efkey0 = ot + "#" + field;
            let efdesc_found0 = self.extern_field_descs.get(efkey0);
            let eftype_found0 = self.extern_field_type_names.get(efkey0);
            if let Some { value: efdesc0 } = efdesc_found0 {
                if let Some { value: eftype0 } = eftype_found0 {
                    self.mv.visitFieldInsn(180, ebinary0, field, efdesc0); // GETFIELD
                    return eftype0;
                }
            }
            // `recv.field` falling back to a zero-arg `getField`/`isField` instance method (Java
            // bean-getter naming) -- see `Checker.hotc`'s own matching fallback (this file doesn't
            // share its registries, same split as everywhere else). Receiver `ot` is already on
            // the stack (this arm's own caller already `gen_expr`'d it), so this is just an
            // ordinary zero-arg instance call from here.
            let cap0 = capitalize_first_cg(field);
            var pget_known = false;
            var pget_desc0 = "";
            var pget_name = "get" + cap0;
            let getx_found = self.extern_method_descs.get(ot + "#get" + cap0 + "#0");
            if let Some { value: gxd } = getx_found { pget_known = true; pget_desc0 = gxd; }
            if !pget_known {
                pget_name = "is" + cap0;
                let isx_found = self.extern_method_descs.get(ot + "#is" + cap0 + "#0");
                if let Some { value: ixd } = isx_found { pget_known = true; pget_desc0 = ixd; }
            }
            if pget_known {
                let pget_desc = pget_desc0;
                var piface0 = 0;
                let piface0_found = self.extern_is_interface.get(ot);
                if let Some { value: piv0 } = piface0_found { piface0 = piv0; }
                if piface0 == 1 {
                    self.mv.visitMethodInsn(185, ebinary0, pget_name, pget_desc, true); // INVOKEINTERFACE
                } else {
                    self.mv.visitMethodInsn(182, ebinary0, pget_name, pget_desc, false); // INVOKEVIRTUAL
                }
                let pget_rt_found = self.extern_method_rettys.get(ot + "#" + pget_name + "#0");
                if let Some { value: pget_rt } = pget_rt_found {
                    return pget_rt;
                }
                return "Int";
            }
            throw CodegenError::new("codegen: unknown field '" + field + "' on extern class '" + ot + "'");
        }
        let fty = self.field_type_of(ot, field);
        self.mv.visitFieldInsn(180, self.qualify(ot), field, self.resolve_descriptor(fty)); // GETFIELD
        return fty;
    }

    // The ordinary (non-arena) tail of `FieldAssign` codegen -- factored out for the same reason
    // `finish_field_access` was, right above.
    fn finish_field_assign(&mut self, ot: String, field: String, value: &Expr) -> String {
        let ebinary_found1 = self.extern_binary.get(ot);
        if let Some { value: ebinary1 } = ebinary_found1 {
            // See `finish_field_access`'s own header for why the extern-alias case needs its own
            // path here too.
            let efkey1 = ot + "#" + field;
            let efdesc_found1 = self.extern_field_descs.get(efkey1);
            let eftype_found1 = self.extern_field_type_names.get(efkey1);
            if let Some { value: efdesc1 } = efdesc_found1 {
                if let Some { value: eftype1 } = eftype_found1 {
                    self.gen_expr(value); // stack: [objref, value]
                    // `value` is category-2 (64-bit: `Long`/`Double`) for THIS field just as
                    // often as category-1 -- a bare `DUP_X1` (opcode 90) only ever preserves a
                    // category-1 value across the 1-deep `objref` underneath it; a category-2
                    // value needs `DUP2_X1` (93) instead, or the JVM verifier rejects the class
                    // outright ("Bad type on operand stack" at this exact instruction -- found
                    // the hard way via a real `Long`-typed struct field reassignment through
                    // `&mut self`, which hits the ordinary (non-extern) sibling of this same bug
                    // right below). Computed into a plain local first, then ONE `visitInsn` call
                    // -- the equivalent `if { visitInsn(93) } else { visitInsn(90) }` shape, with
                    // two separate call sites, was found NOT to reliably compile through the
                    // then-current self-hosted bootstrap when this fix first landed (a real,
                    // separate self-hosting-bootstrap gap, not a mistake in the opcode logic
                    // itself) -- this single-call-site form verified correctly end to end.
                    var dup_opcode1 = 90; // DUP_X1
                    if eftype1 == "Long" || eftype1 == "Double" {
                        dup_opcode1 = 93; // DUP2_X1
                    }
                    self.mv.visitInsn(dup_opcode1); // stack: [value, objref, value]
                    self.mv.visitFieldInsn(181, ebinary1, field, efdesc1); // PUTFIELD -- consumes [objref, value], leaves [value]
                    return eftype1;
                }
            }
            // `recv.field = value` falling back to a single-arg `setField` instance method (Java
            // bean-setter naming) -- write-side counterpart to `finish_field_access`'s own getter
            // fallback, see that fn's own header. Same DUP_X1-before-the-call shape PUTFIELD uses
            // (so this assignment still evaluates to `value`, the usual "assignment is itself a
            // value-producing expression" convention) -- if the real setter happens to be
            // non-`Unit`-returning (not the usual bean convention, but `extern` trusts whatever
            // was declared), its own return value is popped right back off so the stack still
            // ends up holding exactly `[value]` either way.
            let cap1 = capitalize_first_cg(field);
            let setx_found = self.extern_method_descs.get(ot + "#set" + cap1 + "#1");
            if let Some { value: pset_desc } = setx_found {
                let vty1 = self.gen_expr(value); // stack: [objref, value]
                // Same category-1-vs-2 split as the real-field-desc branch above -- see that
                // one's own header for why a bare `DUP_X1` is wrong for a `Long`/`Double` value,
                // and for why this is a single `visitInsn` call fed by a local, not two separate
                // call sites in each `if`/`else` arm.
                var dup_opcode2 = 90; // DUP_X1
                if vty1 == "Long" || vty1 == "Double" {
                    dup_opcode2 = 93; // DUP2_X1
                }
                self.mv.visitInsn(dup_opcode2); // stack: [value, objref, value] (same shape PUTFIELD's own path uses, right below)
                var piface1 = 0;
                let piface1_found = self.extern_is_interface.get(ot);
                if let Some { value: piv1 } = piface1_found { piface1 = piv1; }
                if piface1 == 1 {
                    self.mv.visitMethodInsn(185, ebinary1, "set" + cap1, pset_desc, true); // INVOKEINTERFACE
                } else {
                    self.mv.visitMethodInsn(182, ebinary1, "set" + cap1, pset_desc, false); // INVOKEVIRTUAL
                }
                var pset_rt = "Unit";
                let pset_rt_found = self.extern_method_rettys.get(ot + "#set" + cap1 + "#1");
                if let Some { value: prtv } = pset_rt_found { pset_rt = prtv; }
                if pset_rt != "Unit" {
                    if pset_rt == "Long" || pset_rt == "Double" {
                        self.mv.visitInsn(88); // POP2
                    } else {
                        self.mv.visitInsn(87); // POP
                    }
                }
                return vty1;
            }
            throw CodegenError::new("codegen: unknown field '" + field + "' on extern class '" + ot + "'");
        }
        let fty = self.field_type_of(ot, field);
        self.gen_expr(value); // stack: [objref, value]
        // A `Long`/`Double`-typed field is a real, previously-undetected gap here: `value` is
        // category-2 (64-bit), so a bare `DUP_X1` (90) -- which only ever correctly preserves a
        // category-1 value across the 1-deep `objref` underneath it -- produces a real,
        // verifier-rejected class ("Bad type on operand stack ... long_2nd ... not assignable to
        // category1 type" at this exact `DUP_X1`). `DUP2_X1` (93) is the real fix for a
        // category-2 value in this exact stack shape (JVM spec `dup2_x1` Form 2: a category-2
        // value1 over a category-1 value2 duplicates as value1, value2, value1 -- exactly
        // `value, objref, value` here). Found the hard way assigning to a `Long`-typed struct
        // field (`self.last_ns = now;`) through `&mut self`.
        var dup_opcode = 90; // DUP_X1
        if fty == "Long" || fty == "Double" {
            dup_opcode = 93; // DUP2_X1
        }
        self.mv.visitInsn(dup_opcode); // stack: [value, objref, value]
        self.mv.visitFieldInsn(181, self.qualify(ot), field, self.resolve_descriptor(fty)); // PUTFIELD -- consumes [objref, value], leaves [value]
        return fty;
    }

    // The real user-defined-struct-method/dyn-interface/extern-method/native-String-fallback
    // dispatch body of the old inline `MethodCall` arm -- factored out for the same reason
    // `finish_field_access`/`finish_field_assign` were, right above, so `gen_safe_call` (`x?.method(
    // ...)`, see `Ast.hc`'s own `Expr.SafeCall` header) can reuse it after its own `DUP`/`IFNULL`
    // prelude, with the receiver ALREADY on the stack -- same precondition the plain `MethodCall`
    // arm's own body already assumed (right after `self.gen_expr(recv)` pushed it).
    // Emits `INVOKEVIRTUAL Vec$<elem_type>.push` -- the receiver (a `Vec$<elem_type>` reference)
    // and the value to push must already be on the stack, in that order, exactly like any other
    // ordinary method call's own operands. Shared by `World#spawn`'s own codegen (right below) for
    // every `.push(...)` it emits -- one per archetype field, plus one for the entity id.
    fn gen_vec_push_ecs(&mut self, elem_type: String) {
        let vec_class = "Vec$" + elem_type;
        let pkey = vec_class + "#push";
        var pdesc = "";
        let pdesc_found = self.struct_methods.get(pkey);
        if let Some { value: pd } = pdesc_found { pdesc = pd; }
        self.mv.visitMethodInsn(182, self.qualify(vec_class), "push", pdesc, false); // INVOKEVIRTUAL
    }

    // `gen_vec_push_ecs`'s own `.length()` sibling -- receiver already on the stack, leaves the
    // real `Int` result. Used by `World#spawn`'s own codegen to compute the row an entity's data
    // just landed at (`entities.length() - 1`, right after that push).
    fn gen_vec_length_ecs(&mut self, elem_type: String) {
        let vec_class = "Vec$" + elem_type;
        let lkey = vec_class + "#length";
        var ldesc = "";
        let ldesc_found = self.struct_methods.get(lkey);
        if let Some { value: ld } = ldesc_found { ldesc = ld; }
        self.mv.visitMethodInsn(182, self.qualify(vec_class), "length", ldesc, false); // INVOKEVIRTUAL
    }

    fn finish_method_call(&mut self, rt: String, method: String, args: Vec<Expr>) -> String {
        // `world.set_resource(DeltaTime { seconds: 0.016 })` -- `Checker.hotc`'s own `World#
        // set_resource` gate already validated the single argument is a real resource struct
        // literal. Real bytecode: pop the unused `World` receiver (same real, previously-hit
        // stack-leak fix `World#spawn` right below already needed -- `World` itself carries no
        // state, see `stdlib/ecs.hotc`'s own header), evaluate the resource value, PUTSTATIC it
        // straight into `__resource_<Name>` (the field `compile_program`'s own resource-field-
        // declaration loop already declared, one per distinct `resource` type in the program --
        // see `resource_field_name_ecs`'s own header).
        if rt == "World" && method == "set_resource" {
            self.mv.visitInsn(87); // POP the unused receiver
            let arg0 = args.get(0);
            var res_name = "";
            match arg0 {
                StructLit { name, field_names, field_values, generic_base } => { res_name = name; }
                _ => {}
            }
            let arg0_2 = args.get(0); // fresh copy -- the `match` above already consumed the first one
            self.gen_expr(arg0_2);
            self.mv.visitFieldInsn(179, self.owner, resource_field_name_ecs(res_name), self.resolve_descriptor(res_name)); // PUTSTATIC
            return "Unit";
        }
        // `world.spawn(Transform { ... }, Velocity { ... }, ...)` -- see `ECS_IDEAS.md`'s own
        // design doc. `Checker.hotc`'s own `World#spawn` gate already validated every arg is a
        // real component struct literal by the time this ever runs, so they're trusted here
        // without re-checking -- this fn's whole job is emitting the real bytecode: push the new
        // row's entity id onto the archetype's own `entities` `Vec<Int>`, push each component
        // value onto its own matching `Vec<Component>` field (`archetype_field_name_ecs`'s own
        // positional `c0`/`c1`/... naming, same order `synthesize_archetype_struct_ecs` used), then
        // bump the shared, program-wide entity id counter. The archetype STRUCT/STATIC themselves
        // were already synthesized ahead of time by `compile_program` (see that fn's own ECS
        // section) -- this only ever REFERENCES them, by the same deterministic name.
        if rt == "World" && method == "spawn" {
            // `self.gen_expr(recv)` (the `MethodCall` arm's own caller, right above this fn)
            // already pushed the real `World` instance's object reference onto the stack before
            // ever reaching here -- but `World` carries no state at all (see `stdlib/ecs.hotc`'s
            // own header), so nothing below ever reads it. A real, previously-undetected stack
            // leak without this `POP`: every `world.spawn(...)` call site left one extra
            // reference sitting under this whole branch's own pushes, silently growing the
            // operand stack by one slot per call and eventually desyncing local-slot numbering
            // for anything generated after it in the same method.
            self.mv.visitInsn(87); // POP the unused receiver
            var sig = empty_str_vec4();
            var ai = 0;
            while ai < args.length() {
                match args.get(ai) {
                    StructLit { name, field_names, field_values, generic_base } => { sig.push(name); }
                    _ => { throw CodegenError::new("codegen: 'world.spawn(...)' argument " + JString::valueOf(ai + 1) + " isn't a component struct literal -- Checker.hotc should have already rejected this"); }
                }
                ai = ai + 1;
            }
            let struct_name = archetype_struct_name_ecs(&sig);
            let static_name = archetype_static_name_ecs(&sig);
            let struct_desc = "L" + self.qualify(struct_name) + ";";
            // The new entity's own id, captured into a raw, unregistered local slot (same "read
            // once, reuse the stored copy" trick `gen_arena_field_set`'s own temp slot already
            // uses) -- needed at the END too, both to bump the counter (reading the OLD value, not
            // the already-incremented one) and to leave THIS call's own result value on the stack
            // (`world.spawn(...)` now returns the entity id it just created, so `add`/`remove`
            // have something to operate on later -- see `ECS_IDEAS.md`'s own "Archetype-transition
            // mechanics").
            self.mv.visitFieldInsn(178, self.owner, "__next_entity_id", "I"); // GETSTATIC counter
            let eid_slot = self.next_slot;
            self.next_slot = self.next_slot + 1;
            self.mv.visitVarInsn(store_opcode("Int"), eid_slot); // ISTORE
            // `entities.push(<the new entity id>)`.
            self.mv.visitFieldInsn(178, self.owner, static_name, struct_desc); // GETSTATIC archetype
            self.mv.visitFieldInsn(180, self.qualify(struct_name), "entities", self.resolve_descriptor("Vec$Int")); // GETFIELD entities
            self.mv.visitVarInsn(load_opcode("Int"), eid_slot); // ILOAD
            self.gen_vec_push_ecs("Int");
            // `c{i}.push(component_value)` for each component, in signature order, plus
            // `c{i}_changed.push(true)` right alongside it -- a freshly spawned row reads as
            // "changed" for every one of its own components (see `archetype_field_changed_name_
            // ecs`'s own header: `Changed<T>` means "written, or the row was created/moved-into,
            // since the start of the current `world.run_all()` call").
            var ci = 0;
            while ci < sig.length() {
                let comp_type = sig.get(ci);
                let field_name = archetype_field_name_ecs(ci);
                self.mv.visitFieldInsn(178, self.owner, static_name, struct_desc); // GETSTATIC archetype
                self.mv.visitFieldInsn(180, self.qualify(struct_name), field_name, self.resolve_descriptor("Vec$" + comp_type)); // GETFIELD c{i}
                self.gen_expr(args.get(ci));
                self.gen_vec_push_ecs(comp_type);
                self.mv.visitFieldInsn(178, self.owner, static_name, struct_desc); // GETSTATIC archetype
                self.mv.visitFieldInsn(180, self.qualify(struct_name), archetype_field_changed_name_ecs(ci), self.resolve_descriptor("Vec$Bool")); // GETFIELD c{i}_changed
                self.mv.visitInsn(4); // ICONST_1 (true)
                self.gen_vec_push_ecs("Bool");
                ci = ci + 1;
            }
            // `__next_entity_id = <the new entity id> + 1;`
            self.mv.visitVarInsn(load_opcode("Int"), eid_slot); // ILOAD
            self.mv.visitIntInsn(17, 1); // SIPUSH 1
            self.mv.visitInsn(96); // IADD
            self.mv.visitFieldInsn(179, self.owner, "__next_entity_id", "I"); // PUTSTATIC counter
            // `__entity_archetype_id.push(<archetype>.__id);` -- the archetype's own compile-time-
            // assigned id, read back as a real field (see `synthesize_archetype_struct_ecs`'s own
            // header) rather than needing a second, Codegen-instance-level lookup table here.
            self.mv.visitFieldInsn(178, self.owner, "__entity_archetype_id", self.resolve_descriptor("Vec$Int")); // GETSTATIC
            self.mv.visitFieldInsn(178, self.owner, static_name, struct_desc); // GETSTATIC archetype
            self.mv.visitFieldInsn(180, self.qualify(struct_name), "__id", "I"); // GETFIELD
            self.gen_vec_push_ecs("Int");
            // `__entity_row.push(<archetype>.entities.length() - 1);` -- the row this entity's own
            // data just landed at (one past every row that existed before this push).
            self.mv.visitFieldInsn(178, self.owner, "__entity_row", self.resolve_descriptor("Vec$Int")); // GETSTATIC
            self.mv.visitFieldInsn(178, self.owner, static_name, struct_desc); // GETSTATIC archetype
            self.mv.visitFieldInsn(180, self.qualify(struct_name), "entities", self.resolve_descriptor("Vec$Int")); // GETFIELD entities
            self.gen_vec_length_ecs("Int");
            self.mv.visitIntInsn(17, 1); // SIPUSH 1
            self.mv.visitInsn(100); // ISUB
            self.gen_vec_push_ecs("Int");
            self.mv.visitVarInsn(load_opcode("Int"), eid_slot); // ILOAD -- this call's own result value
            return "Int";
        }
        // `world.run_all()` -- a thin forward to `__world_run_all`, the ONE real fn `compile_
        // program`'s own ECS section synthesizes for the whole program (see that section's own
        // header, and `build_run_all_body_ecs`'s, for how its body -- one `For` loop per (system,
        // matching archetype) pair, in `checker.system_order` -- gets built entirely as real AST
        // and compiled by the ORDINARY fn-codegen pipeline, no hand-written bytecode needed for
        // any of it). Always synthesized, even with zero systems/archetypes (an empty body, a
        // real no-op), so this call is unconditional -- no need to check either count here.
        if rt == "World" && method == "run_all" {
            self.mv.visitInsn(87); // POP the unused receiver -- same reasoning as `World#spawn`'s own POP, right above
            let rdesc_found = self.fn_descs.get("__world_run_all");
            var rdesc = "";
            if let Some { value: rd } = rdesc_found { rdesc = rd; }
            self.mv.visitMethodInsn(184, self.owner, "__world_run_all", rdesc, false); // INVOKESTATIC
            return "Unit";
        }
        // `world.shutdown()` -- thin forward to `__world_shutdown`, same shape as `run_all`'s
        // own. See `Checker.hotc`'s own `World#shutdown` gate, and `build_world_shutdown_body_
        // ecs`'s header, for why this is a real no-op on a program that never needed `__world_
        // pool` at all, and a real, necessary call on one that did (Phase B's dispatch pool is
        // now REUSED across every `world.run_all()` call rather than recreated each time, so
        // nothing inside `run_all` itself can shut it down anymore).
        if rt == "World" && method == "shutdown" {
            self.mv.visitInsn(87); // POP the unused receiver
            let sdesc_found = self.fn_descs.get("__world_shutdown");
            var sdesc = "";
            if let Some { value: sd } = sdesc_found { sdesc = sd; }
            self.mv.visitMethodInsn(184, self.owner, "__world_shutdown", sdesc, false); // INVOKESTATIC
            return "Unit";
        }
        // `world.add(entity, Component { ... })`/`world.remove(entity, Component)` -- see
        // `ECS_IDEAS.md`'s own "Archetype-transition mechanics". Both are thin forwards to one
        // synthesized `__world_add_<Component>`/`__world_remove_<Component>` fn per component
        // type ever added/removed anywhere in the program (`synthesize_transition_fn_ecs`,
        // `compile_program`'s own ECS section) -- same "materialize the real move logic as
        // ordinary `Ast.hc`, forward to it from here" split `world.run_all()` already uses. The
        // component TYPE is read straight off this call site's own args (a `StructLit`'s name for
        // `add`, a bare `Ident`'s name for `remove`) -- `Checker.hotc`'s own gate already
        // validated both shapes, so they're trusted here without re-checking.
        if rt == "World" && method == "add" {
            self.mv.visitInsn(87); // POP the unused receiver
            var add_comp_type = "";
            match args.get(1) {
                StructLit { name, field_names, field_values, generic_base } => { add_comp_type = name; }
                _ => { throw CodegenError::new("codegen: 'world.add(...)' argument 2 isn't a component struct literal -- Checker.hotc should have already rejected this"); }
            }
            self.gen_expr(args.get(0)); // entity id
            self.gen_expr(args.get(1)); // component value
            let add_target = "__world_add_" + add_comp_type;
            let add_desc_found = self.fn_descs.get(add_target);
            var add_desc = "";
            if let Some { value: ad } = add_desc_found { add_desc = ad; }
            self.mv.visitMethodInsn(184, self.owner, add_target, add_desc, false); // INVOKESTATIC
            return "Unit";
        }
        if rt == "World" && method == "remove" {
            self.mv.visitInsn(87); // POP the unused receiver
            var remove_comp_type = "";
            match args.get(1) {
                Ident { name } => { remove_comp_type = name; }
                _ => { throw CodegenError::new("codegen: 'world.remove(...)' argument 2 isn't a bare component type name -- Checker.hotc should have already rejected this"); }
            }
            self.gen_expr(args.get(0)); // entity id
            let remove_target = "__world_remove_" + remove_comp_type;
            let remove_desc_found = self.fn_descs.get(remove_target);
            var remove_desc = "";
            if let Some { value: rd2 } = remove_desc_found { remove_desc = rd2; }
            self.mv.visitMethodInsn(184, self.owner, remove_target, remove_desc, false); // INVOKESTATIC
            return "Unit";
        }
        let mkey = rt + "#" + method;
        let mdesc_found = self.struct_methods.get(mkey);
        if let Some { value: mdesc } = mdesc_found {
            // Real gap found building Phoenix Flight's own `pool.spawn(|| ...)`: a lambda literal
            // passed to a PLAIN struct method's own `&dyn Interface` param used to reach
            // `gen_expr` with no idea what interface it's supposed to implement (this loop just
            // called `gen_expr` on every arg unconditionally) -- `gen_expr`'s own bare `Lambda`
            // arm has no fallback for that, so it threw "lambda literal used outside a valid
            // call-argument context" even for exactly the shape `&dyn Interface` exists to
            // support. Mirrors the extern-method branch's own identical lambda-aware arg loop,
            // right below, against `struct_method_param_types` instead of
            // `extern_method_param_types`.
            var raw_struct_param_types = empty_str_vec4();
            let rspt_found = self.struct_method_param_types.get(mkey);
            if let Some { value: rspt } = rspt_found { raw_struct_param_types = rspt; }
            var mi = 0;
            while mi < args.length() {
                let a2 = args.get(mi);
                match a2 {
                    Lambda { params, body } => {
                        var starget_ty = "";
                        if mi < raw_struct_param_types.length() { starget_ty = raw_struct_param_types.get(mi); }
                        // Phoenix Flight's own compile-time concurrency safety -- see README's own
                        // "Compile-time concurrency safety" section. A lambda passed anywhere as a
                        // `&dyn PhoenixCallable` runs on a REAL background thread eventually --
                        // capturing a plain (non-`@sendable`) struct/extern reference into it is
                        // exactly the class of data-race bug this checks for. Gated on the TARGET
                        // PARAMETER's own declared type, not a specific (receiver, method) pair --
                        // originally hardcoded to `rt == "PhoenixPool" && method == "spawn"`, which
                        // silently let ANY other method taking the same `&dyn PhoenixCallable`
                        // shape (`spawn_after`, say) skip this check entirely just by having a
                        // different name; a real, previously-hit gap once a second such method
                        // (`spawn_after`) actually existed. `params` is reused as the exclude-list
                        // AFTER already being consumed by `collect_lambda_captures` below -- and
                        // `body` likewise reused again by `gen_lambda` right after THIS check --
                        // both safe in practice (this language's own structs are reference types
                        // under the hood, and move-checking isn't enforced at all yet, see
                        // "Status" near the top of the README), but genuinely relying on that
                        // non-enforcement rather than working around it, a real, disclosed
                        // shortcut.
                        if is_dyn_type_name_cg(starget_ty) && is_phoenix_background_iface_cg(dyn_interface_name_cg(starget_ty)) {
                            var captured = empty_str_vec4();
                            self.collect_lambda_captures(body, &params, &mut captured);
                            var cci = 0;
                            while cci < captured.length() {
                                let cname = captured.get(cci);
                                let cty_found = self.lookup_type(cname);
                                if let Some { value: cty } = cty_found {
                                    if !is_sendable_type_cg(cty, &self.sendable_structs) {
                                        throw CodegenError::new("capturing '" + cname + "' (type " + cty + ") into a background task isn't allowed -- background tasks can only capture Int/Bool/Long/Float/Double/String or a struct marked '@sendable' (see README's own \"Compile-time concurrency safety\" section)");
                                    }
                                }
                                cci = cci + 1;
                            }
                        }
                        self.gen_lambda(params, body, starget_ty);
                    }
                    ExternStaticFieldGet { type_name, field } => {
                        if self.is_method_ref_candidate_cg(type_name, field) {
                            var starget_tyb = "";
                            if mi < raw_struct_param_types.length() { starget_tyb = raw_struct_param_types.get(mi); }
                            self.gen_method_ref(type_name, field, starget_tyb);
                        } else {
                            self.gen_expr(a2);
                        }
                    }
                    _ => { self.gen_expr(a2); }
                }
                mi = mi + 1;
            }
            // A `&dyn InterfaceName`-typed receiver (`rt` == `"dyn InterfaceName"`, see
            // `Parser.hotc`'s own `type_name_ref` header) dispatches via a real `INVOKEINTERFACE`
            // (185, `isInterface = true`) against the INTERFACE's own binary name -- not
            // `INVOKEVIRTUAL` against the literal string `"dyn InterfaceName"`, which isn't a real
            // class at all. The method table lookup above already found this entry regardless of
            // which case this is -- see `build_program_info`'s own header for why interface methods
            // share the exact same `method_keys` table struct methods use, keyed `"dyn Name#method"`.
            if is_dyn_type_name_cg(rt) {
                // The interface's OWN binary name -- its declared name for a local interface
                // (unchanged), or the real JVM interface's own binary name for an `extern interface`
                // (see `resolve_descriptor`'s own header, same `self.extern_binary` lookup).
                var iface_owner = dyn_interface_name_cg(rt);
                let extern_iface_owner_found = self.extern_binary.get(iface_owner);
                if let Some { value: eio } = extern_iface_owner_found {
                    iface_owner = eio;
                } else {
                    iface_owner = self.qualify(iface_owner);
                }
                self.mv.visitMethodInsn(185, iface_owner, method, mdesc, true); // INVOKEINTERFACE
            } else {
                self.mv.visitMethodInsn(182, self.qualify(rt), method, mdesc, false); // INVOKEVIRTUAL
            }
            let mret_found = self.struct_method_rettys.get(mkey);
            if let Some { value: mret } = mret_found {
                return mret;
            }
            return "Int";
        }
        let ebinary_found = self.extern_binary.get(rt);
        if let Some { value: ebinary } = ebinary_found {
            // Generate arg bytecode FIRST (receiver `rt` is already on the stack from the caller),
            // collecting each arg's own resolved type as we go -- needed to build the precise,
            // full-signature lookup key BEFORE emitting the actual invoke instruction. See
            // `join_type_names_cg`'s own header: this key disambiguates same-arity overloads
            // (arity-only key is still tried as a fallback, unchanged from before).
            let emkey0 = rt + "#" + method + "#" + arity_str_cg(args.length());
            var earg_types = empty_str_vec4();
            if args_has_lambda_cg(&args) {
                // A `Lambda` arg can't be `gen_expr`'d without knowing its target type first (see
                // `Expr.Lambda`'s own header) -- resolve the callee's REAL declared param types via
                // the arity-only key BEFORE evaluating any arg, same "resolve signature first"
                // restructuring `Checker.hotc`'s own `check_method_call` already needed for the
                // identical reason. Skips the precise-signature-key attempt this branch normally
                // tries first (below) -- a lambda arg has no real type to build one from anyway, and
                // overloading specifically on "lambda vs. not" at one param position isn't a real
                // shape any extern declaration in this codebase has.
                var raw_param_types = empty_str_vec4();
                let rpt_found = self.extern_method_param_types.get(emkey0);
                if let Some { value: rpt } = rpt_found { raw_param_types = rpt; }
                var ei = 0;
                while ei < args.length() {
                    let a = args.get(ei);
                    var at = "";
                    match a {
                        Lambda { params, body } => {
                            var target_ty = "";
                            if ei < raw_param_types.length() { target_ty = raw_param_types.get(ei); }
                            // Same check, same reasoning, as the plain-struct-method branch's own
                            // (right above this fn's `mkey`-keyed half) -- gated on the TARGET
                            // PARAMETER's declared type so `pool.exec.submit(|| ...)` (calling the
                            // underlying extern method DIRECTLY, bypassing `PhoenixPool::spawn`
                            // entirely) can't silently skip it either. A real, previously-
                            // unguarded gap: nothing stopped this call shape before, since the
                            // struct-method branch's own check never covers an EXTERN method call
                            // at all.
                            if is_dyn_type_name_cg(target_ty) && is_phoenix_background_iface_cg(dyn_interface_name_cg(target_ty)) {
                                var captured2 = empty_str_vec4();
                                self.collect_lambda_captures(body, &params, &mut captured2);
                                var cci2 = 0;
                                while cci2 < captured2.length() {
                                    let cname2 = captured2.get(cci2);
                                    let cty_found2 = self.lookup_type(cname2);
                                    if let Some { value: cty2 } = cty_found2 {
                                        if !is_sendable_type_cg(cty2, &self.sendable_structs) {
                                            throw CodegenError::new("capturing '" + cname2 + "' (type " + cty2 + ") into a background task isn't allowed -- background tasks can only capture Int/Bool/Long/Float/Double/String or a struct marked '@sendable' (see README's own \"Compile-time concurrency safety\" section)");
                                        }
                                    }
                                    cci2 = cci2 + 1;
                                }
                            }
                            at = self.gen_lambda(params, body, target_ty);
                        }
                        ExternStaticFieldGet { type_name, field } => {
                            if self.is_method_ref_candidate_cg(type_name, field) {
                                var target_tyb = "";
                                if ei < raw_param_types.length() { target_tyb = raw_param_types.get(ei); }
                                at = self.gen_method_ref(type_name, field, target_tyb);
                            } else {
                                at = self.gen_expr(a);
                            }
                        }
                        _ => { at = self.gen_expr(a); }
                    }
                    earg_types.push(at);
                    ei = ei + 1;
                }
            } else {
                var ei = 0;
                while ei < args.length() {
                    let at = self.gen_expr(args.get(ei));
                    earg_types.push(at);
                    ei = ei + 1;
                }
            }
            let emkey = emkey0;
            let sig_emkey = emkey + "#" + join_type_names_cg(earg_types);
            var edesc_found = self.extern_method_descs.get(sig_emkey);
            var e_known = false;
            var lookup_key = sig_emkey;
            if let Some { value: d1 } = edesc_found { e_known = true; }
            if !e_known {
                edesc_found = self.extern_method_descs.get(emkey);
                lookup_key = emkey;
            }
            var edesc = "";
            if let Some { value: d } = edesc_found {
                edesc = d;
                e_known = true;
            }
            if !e_known {
                throw CodegenError::new("codegen: unknown method '" + method + "' on extern class '" + rt + "'");
            }
            // `is_interface` (see `Ast.hc`'s own `ExternClassDecl` header) picks a real
            // `INVOKEINTERFACE` (185, `isInterface = true`) over the usual `INVOKEVIRTUAL` (182) --
            // required, not cosmetic: the JVM verifier rejects an INVOKEVIRTUAL whose owner is
            // actually an interface.
            var rt_is_iface = 0;
            let iface_found = self.extern_is_interface.get(rt);
            if let Some { value: ifv } = iface_found { rt_is_iface = ifv; }
            if rt_is_iface == 1 {
                self.mv.visitMethodInsn(185, ebinary, method, edesc, true); // INVOKEINTERFACE
            } else {
                self.mv.visitMethodInsn(182, ebinary, method, edesc, false); // INVOKEVIRTUAL
            }
            let ert_found = self.extern_method_rettys.get(lookup_key);
            if let Some { value: ert } = ert_found {
                return ert;
            }
            return "Int";
        }
        if rt != "String" {
            // `extend Target { fn m(&self, ...) { ... } }`'s own desugared top-level fn -- see
            // `Ast.hc`'s own `ExtendDecl` header and `materialize_extend_fns`'s own (this file) for
            // the full story. The receiver's value is ALREADY on the stack (the caller's own
            // `self.gen_expr(recv)`) -- exactly the extension fn's own first (`"self"`) param slot,
            // so this is just an ordinary same-class static call from here,
            // `self.fn_descs`/`self.fn_param_types`/`self.fn_ret_tys` reused directly (the same
            // registries an ordinary top-level `Call` uses). Arity-checked against the desugared
            // param count (self + args) before committing to this as a real match -- a same-named,
            // unrelated top-level fn (which the checker's own `check_extension_fallback` would have
            // already silently rejected as a non-match) falls through to the ordinary "not
            // implemented" error below instead of emitting a wrong-arity `INVOKESTATIC`.
            let ext_desc_found = self.fn_descs.get(method);
            var ext_known = false;
            if let Some { value: extd } = ext_desc_found { ext_known = true; }
            if ext_known {
                var ext_param_types = empty_str_vec4();
                let ext_pt_found = self.fn_param_types.get(method);
                if let Some { value: extpt } = ext_pt_found { ext_param_types = extpt; }
                if ext_param_types.length() == args.length() + 1 {
                    var exi = 0;
                    while exi < args.length() {
                        self.gen_expr(args.get(exi));
                        exi = exi + 1;
                    }
                    let ext_desc = self.fn_descs.get(method);
                    var ext_desc_str = "";
                    if let Some { value: eds } = ext_desc { ext_desc_str = eds; }
                    self.mv.visitMethodInsn(184, self.owner, method, ext_desc_str, false); // INVOKESTATIC
                    let ext_rt_found = self.fn_ret_tys.get(method);
                    if let Some { value: ext_rt } = ext_rt_found {
                        return ext_rt;
                    }
                    return "Int";
                }
            }
            throw CodegenError::new("codegen for 'MethodCall' to '" + method + "' not implemented this phase (receiver was " + rt + ", not String, and no user-defined method by that name exists on it)");
        }
        let argc = args.length();
        if method == "length" {
            if argc != 0 { throw CodegenError::new("'length' takes no arguments"); }
            self.mv.visitMethodInsn(182, "java/lang/String", "length", "()I", false);
            return "Int";
        } else if method == "toUpperCase" {
            if argc != 0 { throw CodegenError::new("'toUpperCase' takes no arguments"); }
            self.mv.visitMethodInsn(182, "java/lang/String", "toUpperCase", "()Ljava/lang/String;", false);
            return "String";
        } else if method == "toLowerCase" {
            if argc != 0 { throw CodegenError::new("'toLowerCase' takes no arguments"); }
            self.mv.visitMethodInsn(182, "java/lang/String", "toLowerCase", "()Ljava/lang/String;", false);
            return "String";
        } else if method == "trim" {
            if argc != 0 { throw CodegenError::new("'trim' takes no arguments"); }
            self.mv.visitMethodInsn(182, "java/lang/String", "trim", "()Ljava/lang/String;", false);
            return "String";
        } else if method == "contains" {
            if argc != 1 { throw CodegenError::new("'contains' takes exactly one argument"); }
            self.gen_expr(args.get(0));
            self.mv.visitMethodInsn(182, "java/lang/String", "contains", "(Ljava/lang/CharSequence;)Z", false);
            return "Bool";
        } else if method == "indexOf" {
            if argc != 1 { throw CodegenError::new("'indexOf' takes exactly one argument"); }
            self.gen_expr(args.get(0));
            self.mv.visitMethodInsn(182, "java/lang/String", "indexOf", "(Ljava/lang/String;)I", false);
            return "Int";
        } else if method == "codePointAt" {
            if argc != 1 { throw CodegenError::new("'codePointAt' takes exactly one argument"); }
            self.gen_expr(args.get(0));
            self.mv.visitMethodInsn(182, "java/lang/String", "codePointAt", "(I)I", false);
            return "Int";
        } else if method == "substring" {
            if argc != 2 { throw CodegenError::new("'substring' takes exactly two arguments"); }
            self.gen_expr(args.get(0));
            self.gen_expr(args.get(1));
            self.mv.visitMethodInsn(182, "java/lang/String", "substring", "(II)Ljava/lang/String;", false);
            return "String";
        } else if method == "split" {
            if argc != 1 { throw CodegenError::new("'split' takes exactly one argument"); }
            self.gen_expr(args.get(0));
            self.mv.visitMethodInsn(182, "java/lang/String", "split", "(Ljava/lang/String;)[Ljava/lang/String;", false);
            return "[String]";
        } else if method == "isEmpty" {
            if argc != 0 { throw CodegenError::new("'isEmpty' takes no arguments"); }
            self.mv.visitMethodInsn(182, "java/lang/String", "isEmpty", "()Z", false);
            return "Bool";
        } else {
            throw CodegenError::new("codegen for String method '" + method + "' not implemented this phase");
        }
    }

    // `x?.method(args)` -- see `Ast.hc`'s own `Expr.SafeCall` header. Pushes `recv`, `DUP`s it,
    // `IFNULL`s straight to a null-result label -- the non-null path reuses `finish_method_call`
    // (receiver already on the stack, same precondition the plain `MethodCall` arm gives it) and
    // args are only evaluated on THAT path, a real short-circuit (an arg with a side effect never
    // runs when `recv` is null). Both paths leave exactly one value on the stack at the merge point
    // (`end_label`) -- the checker's own `wrap_safe_result` already guarantees the method's return
    // type is reference-shaped, never a primitive, so this never needs to reconcile a primitive
    // against a null reference.
    fn gen_safe_call(&mut self, recv: &Expr, method: String, args: Vec<Expr>) -> String {
        let rt = self.gen_expr(recv);
        self.mv.visitInsn(89); // DUP
        var null_label = Label::new();
        var end_label = Label::new();
        self.mv.visitJumpInsn(198, &null_label); // IFNULL -- pops one; jumps if null; stack: [recv]
        var base = rt;
        if is_nullable_type_name_cg(rt) { base = strip_nullable_cg(rt); }
        let result_ty = self.finish_method_call(base, method, args);
        self.mv.visitJumpInsn(167, &end_label); // GOTO
        self.mv.visitLabel(&null_label);
        // Real bug found running this for real: leaving the DUP'd `recv` (still typed as the
        // RECEIVER's own class, e.g. `Player`) here, while the non-null path leaves the member's
        // own (generally DIFFERENT) result type, forces ASM's `COMPUTE_FRAMES` to compute a real
        // common supertype for the two at `end_label` -- its default `getCommonSuperClass` does
        // that via `Class.forName` on the actual JVM classpath, which fails outright for a
        // struct/extern type still being compiled IN THIS SAME PASS (`ClassNotFoundException:
        // Player`, not a bug in the struct itself). Fixed by discarding that typed null and
        // pushing a fresh, UNTYPED `ACONST_NULL` instead -- the verifier's "null type" merges with
        // any reference type for free, no reflection needed at all.
        self.mv.visitInsn(87); // POP -- discard the receiver-typed null
        self.mv.visitInsn(1); // ACONST_NULL -- untyped null, safe to merge with any reference type
        self.mv.visitLabel(&end_label);
        if is_nullable_type_name_cg(result_ty) { return result_ty; }
        return result_ty + "?";
    }

    // `x?.field` -- see `Ast.hc`'s own `Expr.SafeFieldAccess` header. Mirrors `gen_safe_call`'s own
    // header exactly, just against `finish_field_access` instead of a method call.
    fn gen_safe_field_access(&mut self, recv: &Expr, field: String) -> String {
        let rt = self.gen_expr(recv);
        self.mv.visitInsn(89); // DUP
        var null_label = Label::new();
        var end_label = Label::new();
        self.mv.visitJumpInsn(198, &null_label); // IFNULL
        var base = rt;
        if is_nullable_type_name_cg(rt) { base = strip_nullable_cg(rt); }
        let result_ty = self.finish_field_access(base, field);
        self.mv.visitJumpInsn(167, &end_label); // GOTO
        self.mv.visitLabel(&null_label);
        // See `gen_safe_call`'s own header for why a fresh, untyped `ACONST_NULL` is needed here
        // instead of leaving the receiver-typed null on the stack.
        self.mv.visitInsn(87); // POP
        self.mv.visitInsn(1); // ACONST_NULL
        self.mv.visitLabel(&end_label);
        if is_nullable_type_name_cg(result_ty) { return result_ty; }
        return result_ty + "?";
    }

    // `x ?: default` -- see `Ast.hc`'s own `Expr.Elvis` header. Real bytecode shape: push `left`,
    // `DUP`, `IFNULL` to a label that pops the leftover null and evaluates `default` instead;
    // otherwise (non-null) `left`'s own value is already exactly what's needed, no further work.
    fn gen_elvis(&mut self, left: &Expr, right: &Expr) -> String {
        let lt = self.gen_expr(left);
        self.mv.visitInsn(89); // DUP
        var null_label = Label::new();
        var end_label = Label::new();
        self.mv.visitJumpInsn(198, &null_label); // IFNULL -- pops one; jumps if null; stack: [left]
        self.mv.visitJumpInsn(167, &end_label); // GOTO -- non-null path: [left] is already the result
        self.mv.visitLabel(&null_label);
        self.mv.visitInsn(87); // POP -- discard the leftover null
        let rt = self.gen_expr(right);
        self.mv.visitLabel(&end_label);
        return rt;
    }

    // `true` iff `type_name` is a real enum (plain or a generic mono instantiation) rather than a
    // plain struct -- `struct_fields`/`struct_ftypes` don't distinguish the two at all (both
    // register into the same table), and an enum's own real `tag: Int` field is a pure codegen-
    // time detail (`gen_enum` adds it directly, never recorded in any field-name table), so
    // checking for "tag" among a type's registered fields can never work. `ProgramInfo`'s own
    // `enum_names` (built directly from `enums: Vec<EnumDecl>`, plain and generic-mono alike) is
    // the real signal instead -- see `ProgramInfo`'s own header.
    fn is_enum_type(&mut self, type_name: String) -> Bool {
        let found = self.enum_names.get(type_name);
        if let Some { value: v } = found {
            return true;
        }
        return false;
    }

    // Same logic as the free `descriptor_of_type` function below, but resolving extern aliases
    // via `self.extern_binary` (a `Registry`, built once per codegen unit -- see `build_codegen`)
    // instead of the parallel-`Vec` form free functions without `&self` access have to use.
    // Duplicated rather than shared for that reason, not an oversight.
    fn resolve_descriptor(&mut self, name0: String) -> String {
        // Nullability is purely an HC-level, compile-time concept -- the real JVM descriptor for a
        // nullable reference type is IDENTICAL to its non-null form (every JVM reference is already
        // nullable). Stripped here, once, before any of the real resolution below, rather than
        // teaching every branch (alias lookup, array/dyn/arena special cases, ...) to ignore a
        // trailing "?" itself.
        var name = name0;
        if is_nullable_type_name_cg(name) { name = strip_nullable_cg(name); }
        if name == "String" { return "Ljava/lang/String;"; }
        if name == "Unit" { return "V"; }
        if name == "Int" { return "I"; }
        // Bool intentionally maps to "I", not real "Z": mapping it to "Z" here causes a struct's
        // field declaration (gen_struct, via descriptor_of_type below) and a StructLit ctor call
        // site (gen_expr, via this function) to disagree on the SAME field's descriptor even in a
        // single clean self-host build, producing a VerifyError. Root cause not found; reverted.
        if name == "Bool" { return "I"; }
        if name == "Long" { return "J"; } // see `descriptor_of_type`'s own header
        if name == "Float" { return "F"; }
        if name == "Double" { return "D"; }
        if name == "Char" { return "C"; }
        if name == "Byte" { return "B"; }
        if is_array_type_name_cg(name) {
            let inner = array_elem_type_name_cg(name);
            return "[" + self.resolve_descriptor(inner);
        }
        if is_dyn_type_name_cg(name) {
            // A LOCAL interface's binary name == its declared name (unchanged, "same convention
            // as structs") -- but an `extern interface Name = "java.lang.Runnable" { ... }` binds
            // to a REAL, already-existing JVM interface instead, so its own real binary name has
            // to be substituted in here too. Reuses `self.extern_binary` -- `build_program_info`'s
            // own `alias_names`/`alias_binaries` construction (this fn's sibling free function,
            // `descriptor_of_type`, right below) folds every `extern interface`'s own name/binary
            // pair into the SAME table `extern class` aliases already populate, so this is the
            // only place either resolution path needs to change at all.
            let bare = dyn_interface_name_cg(name);
            let extern_iface_found = self.extern_binary.get(bare);
            if let Some { value: eb } = extern_iface_found {
                return "L" + eb + ";";
            }
            return "L" + self.qualify(bare) + ";";
        }
        if is_arena_type_name_cg(name) {
            return "Ljava/lang/foreign/MemorySegment;"; // real compiler's own Ty.Arena descriptor mapping
        }
        let found = self.extern_binary.get(name);
        if let Some { value: b } = found {
            return "L" + b + ";";
        }
        return "L" + self.qualify(name) + ";";
    }

    // ANEWARRAY's operand is a bare internal class name (no `L`/`;`) -- mirrors `resolve_descriptor`
    // but returns the bare form, resolving extern aliases via `self.extern_binary`.
    fn resolve_elem_class_name(&mut self, ty: String) -> String {
        if ty == "String" { return "java/lang/String"; }
        if is_array_type_name_cg(ty) { return self.resolve_descriptor(ty); }
        let found = self.extern_binary.get(ty);
        if let Some { value: b } = found {
            return b;
        }
        return self.qualify(ty);
    }

    // See the free-function `qualify_names_cg`'s own header (same convention, same fallback) --
    // this is the `&mut self`-method mirror of it, backed by `self.module_of` (a `Registry`, built
    // from the SAME `ProgramInfo.module_names`/`module_values` arrays `qualify_names_cg` scans
    // directly) for every codegen site that already has a `CodeGen` receiver handy (`gen_expr`/
    // `gen_binary`/`gen_stmt`/`gen_variant_construct`) rather than the raw `structs`/`enums`/
    // `interfaces` slices `gen_struct`/`gen_enum`/`gen_interface` work from directly. Deliberately
    // NOT applied to `self.owner` (the synthetic top-level entry class) anywhere -- that stays
    // unqualified/default-package by design, a disclosed narrower scope (see `Ast.hc`'s own
    // `StructDecl.module_name` header).
    fn qualify(&mut self, name: String) -> String {
        var base = name;
        if is_mono_name_cg(name) { base = mono_base_cg(name); }
        let found = self.module_of.get(base);
        if let Some { value: m } = found {
            return dot_to_slash_cg(m) + "/" + name;
        }
        return name;
    }

    // Enum variant construction (`VariantName { ... }`) -- extracted out of `gen_expr`'s own
    // `StructLit` arm so a BARE variant name used as an ordinary value expression (`RETURN`, no
    // braces at all -- real `Lexer.hotc`/`Parser.hotc` usage, e.g. `Token { kind: FN }`/`kind ==
    // RETURN`, treating a fieldless enum's variants like plain named constants) can reuse the
    // exact same construction bytecode via `gen_expr`'s own `Ident` arm, just with empty
    // `field_names`/`field_values` -- see that arm's own header for why this doesn't need any new
    // field-count tracking (`check_struct_lit`/this construction path already don't verify every
    // declared field was actually supplied, an existing, disclosed imprecision this reuses as-is
    // rather than introduces new). `vowner` is the enum's own real class name (already resolved by
    // the caller, either from `generic_base` or a bare-name `variant_owner` lookup).
    // Free-variable analysis for a lambda literal's own body -- every `Ident` reachable inside it
    // that ISN'T one of `exclude` (the lambda's own params, plus any NESTED lambda's own params
    // while walking through one) AND names a real known local (`self.var_types.get` finds it) is a
    // capture: a real enclosing value the synthesized implementer class needs its own field for
    // (see `gen_lambda`'s own header). A plain top-level fn name, an unknown identifier, or one of
    // `exclude`'s own names is never a capture. Recurses through every `Expr` shape that can
    // contain a nested `Expr` -- exhaustive, matching `Ast.hc`'s own `Expr` enum (a genuinely new
    // variant needs a new arm here too, same as `subst_expr_cg`'s own already-exhaustive match).
    fn collect_lambda_captures(&mut self, e: &Expr, exclude: &Vec<String>, out: &mut Vec<String>) {
        match e {
            IntLit { value } => {}
            FloatLit { text } => {}
            DoubleLit { text } => {}
            StringLit { value } => {}
            StringInterp { literals, exprs } => { self.collect_lambda_captures_list(exprs, exclude, out); }
            BoolLit { value } => {}
            Ident { name } => {
                if !str_vec_contains_cg(exclude, name) && !str_vec_contains_cg(out, name) {
                    let v = self.lookup_type(name);
                    if let Some { value: ty } = v { out.push(name); }
                }
            }
            Binary { op, left, right } => { self.collect_lambda_captures(left, exclude, out); self.collect_lambda_captures(right, exclude, out); }
            Unary { op, expr } => { self.collect_lambda_captures(expr, exclude, out); }
            Call { callee, args } => { self.collect_lambda_captures_list(args, exclude, out); }
            FieldAccess { obj, field } => { self.collect_lambda_captures(obj, exclude, out); }
            MethodCall { recv, method, args } => { self.collect_lambda_captures(recv, exclude, out); self.collect_lambda_captures_list(args, exclude, out); }
            Assign { name, value } => {
                if !str_vec_contains_cg(exclude, name) && !str_vec_contains_cg(out, name) {
                    let v = self.lookup_type(name);
                    if let Some { value: ty } = v { out.push(name); }
                }
                self.collect_lambda_captures(value, exclude, out);
            }
            FieldAssign { obj, field, value } => { self.collect_lambda_captures(obj, exclude, out); self.collect_lambda_captures(value, exclude, out); }
            StructLit { name, field_names, field_values, generic_base } => { self.collect_lambda_captures_list(field_values, exclude, out); }
            ArrayLit { elements } => { self.collect_lambda_captures_list(elements, exclude, out); }
            ArrayRepeat { value, count } => { self.collect_lambda_captures(value, exclude, out); self.collect_lambda_captures(count, exclude, out); }
            ArenaNew { struct_name, count } => { self.collect_lambda_captures(count, exclude, out); }
            IndexFieldGet { arr_name, index, field } => {
                if !str_vec_contains_cg(exclude, arr_name) && !str_vec_contains_cg(out, arr_name) {
                    let v = self.lookup_type(arr_name);
                    if let Some { value: ty } = v { out.push(arr_name); }
                }
                self.collect_lambda_captures(index, exclude, out);
            }
            IndexFieldSet { arr_name, index, field, value } => {
                if !str_vec_contains_cg(exclude, arr_name) && !str_vec_contains_cg(out, arr_name) {
                    let v = self.lookup_type(arr_name);
                    if let Some { value: ty } = v { out.push(arr_name); }
                }
                self.collect_lambda_captures(index, exclude, out);
                self.collect_lambda_captures(value, exclude, out);
            }
            Index { arr, index } => { self.collect_lambda_captures(arr, exclude, out); self.collect_lambda_captures(index, exclude, out); }
            IndexAssign { arr, index, value } => { self.collect_lambda_captures(arr, exclude, out); self.collect_lambda_captures(index, exclude, out); self.collect_lambda_captures(value, exclude, out); }
            StaticCall { type_name, method, args } => { self.collect_lambda_captures_list(args, exclude, out); }
            ExternStaticFieldGet { type_name, field } => { }
            ExternStaticFieldSet { type_name, field, value } => { self.collect_lambda_captures(value, exclude, out); }
            Cast { expr, target_type } => { self.collect_lambda_captures(expr, exclude, out); }
            IsCheck { expr, target_type } => { self.collect_lambda_captures(expr, exclude, out); }
            Lambda { params, body } => {
                var nested_exclude = empty_str_vec4();
                var nei = 0;
                while nei < exclude.length() { nested_exclude.push(exclude.get(nei)); nei = nei + 1; }
                var npi = 0;
                while npi < params.length() { nested_exclude.push(params.get(npi)); npi = npi + 1; }
                self.collect_lambda_captures(body, &nested_exclude, out);
            }
            TryOp { expr } => { self.collect_lambda_captures(expr, exclude, out); }
            NamedArg { name, value } => { self.collect_lambda_captures(value, exclude, out); }
            NullLit => {}
            SafeCall { recv, method, args } => { self.collect_lambda_captures(recv, exclude, out); self.collect_lambda_captures_list(args, exclude, out); }
            SafeFieldAccess { recv, field } => { self.collect_lambda_captures(recv, exclude, out); }
            Elvis { left, right } => { self.collect_lambda_captures(left, exclude, out); self.collect_lambda_captures(right, exclude, out); }
            // `var_name` is a locally-bound loop variable, same shape `Lambda`'s own `params`
            // handling right above already gives -- excluded from `result_expr`/`cond`'s own
            // traversal (never a real capture candidate), but NOT from `iter_expr`'s (evaluated
            // in the OUTER scope, before `var_name` is bound at all).
            Comprehension { result_expr, var_name, iter_expr, cond } => {
                self.collect_lambda_captures(iter_expr, exclude, out);
                var nested_exclude2 = empty_str_vec4();
                var nei2 = 0;
                while nei2 < exclude.length() { nested_exclude2.push(exclude.get(nei2)); nei2 = nei2 + 1; }
                nested_exclude2.push(var_name);
                self.collect_lambda_captures(result_expr, &nested_exclude2, out);
                match cond {
                    Some { value: c } => { self.collect_lambda_captures(c, &nested_exclude2, out); }
                    None => {}
                }
            }
        }
    }

    fn collect_lambda_captures_list(&mut self, es: Vec<Expr>, exclude: &Vec<String>, out: &mut Vec<String>) {
        var i = 0;
        while i < es.length() {
            self.collect_lambda_captures(es.get(i), exclude, out);
            i = i + 1;
        }
    }

    // A fresh `CodeGen` for the synthesized lambda-implementer class's own single method body --
    // reuses every PROGRAM-WIDE registry `self` already built (fn descriptors, struct/extern/
    // generic-inference tables, `interfaces_raw`, `module_of`, ...) unchanged, since those describe
    // the whole compiled program, not anything specific to the ENCLOSING fn/method `self` is
    // currently compiling -- only the per-method state (`mv`, `slots`/`var_types`/`next_slot`,
    // `ret_ty`, loop labels, line tracking) starts fresh, exactly like a real new `build_codegen`
    // call would produce. `owner` stays `self.owner` (the MAIN class), not the synthetic class's
    // own name -- mirrors `gen_method`'s own established convention (a lambda body calling an
    // ordinary top-level fn still needs `INVOKESTATIC` against the class that fn actually lives in).
    fn clone_codegen_for_lambda(&mut self, mv: MethodVisitor2, ret_type: String, fn_identity: String) -> CodeGen {
        var slot_scopes_seed = vec_of(registry_new("__init__", -1));
        var var_type_scopes_seed = vec_of(registry_new("__init__", "Int"));
        return CodeGen {
            mv: mv,
            target: self.target,
            slots: registry_new("__init__", -1),
            slot_scopes: Vec { data: slot_scopes_seed.data, len: 0 },
            next_slot: 0,
            owner: self.owner,
            fn_descs: self.fn_descs,
            fn_ret_tys: self.fn_ret_tys,
            fn_param_types: self.fn_param_types,
            fn_param_names: self.fn_param_names,
            var_types: registry_new("__init__", "Int"),
            var_type_scopes: Vec { data: var_type_scopes_seed.data, len: 0 },
            ret_ty: ret_type,
            struct_fields: self.struct_fields,
            struct_ftypes: self.struct_ftypes,
            struct_methods: self.struct_methods,
            struct_method_rettys: self.struct_method_rettys,
            struct_method_param_types: self.struct_method_param_types,
            sendable_structs: self.sendable_structs,
            variant_owner: self.variant_owner,
            variant_tags: self.variant_tags,
            extern_binary: self.extern_binary,
            extern_is_interface: self.extern_is_interface,
            extern_method_descs: self.extern_method_descs,
            extern_method_rettys: self.extern_method_rettys,
            extern_method_static: self.extern_method_static,
            extern_method_ctor: self.extern_method_ctor,
            extern_method_param_types: self.extern_method_param_types,
            extern_field_descs: self.extern_field_descs,
            extern_field_type_names: self.extern_field_type_names,
            extern_field_static: self.extern_field_static,
            struct_extends: self.struct_extends,
            generic_fn_infer_idx: self.generic_fn_infer_idx,
            generic_fn_infer_unwrap: self.generic_fn_infer_unwrap,
            generic_fn_infer_idx2: self.generic_fn_infer_idx2,
            generic_fn_infer_unwrap2: self.generic_fn_infer_unwrap2,
            generic_struct_infer_field: self.generic_struct_infer_field,
            generic_enum_infer_owner: self.generic_enum_infer_owner,
            generic_enum_infer_field: self.generic_enum_infer_field,
            static_types: self.static_types,
            enum_names: self.enum_names,
            cur_loop_start: Label::new(),
            cur_loop_end: Label::new(),
            variant_field_names: self.variant_field_names,
            variant_field_types: self.variant_field_types,
            last_emitted_line: -1,
            module_of: self.module_of,
            interfaces_raw: self.interfaces_raw,
            fn_identity: fn_identity,
            lambda_counter: 0,
        };
    }

    // A lambda literal (`|params| expr`), checked against `target_type` (always `"dyn
    // InterfaceName"` -- see `Ast.hc`'s own `Expr.Lambda` header and `Checker.hotc`'s own
    // `check_lambda`, which already validated this exact shape). Synthesizes a real, standalone
    // implementer class on the spot -- one real `.class` file per lambda LITERAL (not per call,
    // there's exactly one construction site each compiles to): a field per captured enclosing
    // local, a constructor taking them positionally, and the target interface's one abstract
    // method, whose body loads the captured fields back into fresh locals then evaluates `body`
    // directly. No `invokedynamic`/`LambdaMetafactory` -- this compiler emits direct bytecode the
    // way `javac` itself did for anonymous inner classes before Java 8, matching the "real class
    // per lambda" design `IDEAS.md` always specified for this feature.
    fn gen_lambda(&mut self, params: Vec<String>, body: Expr, target_type: String) -> String {
        let iface_name = dyn_interface_name_cg(target_type);
        var found_method = false;
        var m_name = "";
        var m_params = empty_param_vec2();
        var m_ret = "Unit";
        var ii = 0;
        while ii < self.interfaces_raw.length() && !found_method {
            let ifd = self.interfaces_raw.get(ii);
            let ifd2 = self.interfaces_raw.get(ii); // fresh copy -- `.methods` is only safe to read once
            if ifd.name == iface_name && ifd2.methods.length() == 1 {
                let m = ifd2.methods.get(0);
                m_name = m.name;
                m_params = m.params;
                m_ret = m.ret_type;
                found_method = true;
            }
            ii = ii + 1;
        }
        if !found_method {
            throw CodegenError::new("codegen: lambda literal's target interface '" + iface_name + "' not found or not single-method");
        }
        let iface_binary = struct_iface_jvm_name(iface_name, &self.interfaces_raw);

        var captures = empty_str_vec4();
        var lambda_body_copy = body;
        self.collect_lambda_captures(lambda_body_copy, &params, &mut captures);
        var capture_types = empty_str_vec4();
        var cti = 0;
        while cti < captures.length() {
            capture_types.push(self.type_of(captures.get(cti)));
            cti = cti + 1;
        }

        self.lambda_counter = self.lambda_counter + 1;
        let synth_name = self.owner + "$" + self.fn_identity + "$Lambda" + arity_str_cg(self.lambda_counter);

        // === Build and write the synthesized class ===
        var cw = ClassWriter2::new(2); // COMPUTE_FRAMES
        CodegenShim::visitClassHeader(&mut cw, classfile_major_for_target(self.target), 33, synth_name, "java/lang/Object", [iface_binary; 1]); // ACC_PUBLIC | ACC_SUPER, implements iface_binary
        CodegenShim::visitSource(&mut cw, self.fn_identity + ".hotc");
        var cfi = 0;
        while cfi < captures.length() {
            CodegenShim::visitInstanceField(&mut cw, 1, "cap$" + captures.get(cfi), self.resolve_descriptor(capture_types.get(cfi))); // ACC_PUBLIC
            cfi = cfi + 1;
        }
        // Constructor: one param per capture, positionally, stored into the matching `cap$` field.
        var ctor_desc = "(";
        var cdi = 0;
        while cdi < capture_types.length() {
            ctor_desc = ctor_desc + self.resolve_descriptor(capture_types.get(cdi));
            cdi = cdi + 1;
        }
        ctor_desc = ctor_desc + ")V";
        let ctor = CodegenShim::visitMethodNoSig(&mut cw, 1, "<init>", ctor_desc, ["x"; 0]); // ACC_PUBLIC
        ctor.visitCode();
        ctor.visitVarInsn(25, 0); // ALOAD 0
        ctor.visitMethodInsn(183, "java/lang/Object", "<init>", "()V", false); // INVOKESPECIAL
        var cslot = 1;
        var cci = 0;
        while cci < captures.length() {
            let cty2 = capture_types.get(cci);
            ctor.visitVarInsn(25, 0); // ALOAD 0
            ctor.visitVarInsn(load_opcode(cty2), cslot);
            ctor.visitFieldInsn(181, synth_name, "cap$" + captures.get(cci), self.resolve_descriptor(cty2)); // PUTFIELD
            if cty2 == "Long" || cty2 == "Double" { cslot = cslot + 2; } else { cslot = cslot + 1; }
            cci = cci + 1;
        }
        ctor.visitInsn(177); // RETURN
        ctor.visitMaxs(0, 0);
        ctor.visitEnd();

        // The interface's one abstract method: load every captured field into a fresh local (same
        // NAME the lambda body's own `Ident`s already use, so ordinary `Ident` codegen resolves
        // them with zero special-casing), bind the lambda's own params from the METHOD's real
        // declared param types, evaluate `body`, then return it (or discard it, for a `Unit`-
        // returning target method -- mirrors `ExprStmt`'s own care around that).
        var m_param_names = param_names_of(m_params);
        var m_param_types = param_type_names_of(m_params);
        var mdesc = "(";
        var mdi = 0;
        while mdi < m_param_types.length() {
            mdesc = mdesc + self.resolve_descriptor(m_param_types.get(mdi));
            mdi = mdi + 1;
        }
        mdesc = mdesc + ")" + self.resolve_descriptor(m_ret);
        let mmv = CodegenShim::visitMethodNoSig(&mut cw, 1, m_name, mdesc, ["x"; 0]); // ACC_PUBLIC
        var lgen = self.clone_codegen_for_lambda(mmv, m_ret, self.fn_identity + "$Lambda" + arity_str_cg(self.lambda_counter));
        lgen.alloc_slot("self", synth_name);
        lgen.var_types.register("self", synth_name);
        var lpi = 0;
        while lpi < m_param_names.length() {
            lgen.alloc_slot(m_param_names.get(lpi), m_param_types.get(lpi));
            lgen.var_types.register(m_param_names.get(lpi), m_param_types.get(lpi));
            lpi = lpi + 1;
        }
        // Bind the lambda's OWN param names (which may differ from the interface method's own
        // declared param names) to the SAME slots/types the interface method's real params were
        // just bound to above -- both names now resolve to the identical local.
        var bpi = 0;
        while bpi < params.length() && bpi < m_param_names.length() {
            let real_slot = lgen.slot_of(m_param_names.get(bpi));
            lgen.slots.register(params.get(bpi), real_slot);
            lgen.var_types.register(params.get(bpi), m_param_types.get(bpi));
            bpi = bpi + 1;
        }
        lgen.mv.visitCode();
        var lci = 0;
        while lci < captures.length() {
            let lcty = capture_types.get(lci);
            let lslot = lgen.alloc_slot(captures.get(lci), lcty);
            lgen.var_types.register(captures.get(lci), lcty);
            lgen.mv.visitVarInsn(25, 0); // ALOAD 0 (self, the synthesized instance)
            lgen.mv.visitFieldInsn(180, synth_name, "cap$" + captures.get(lci), lgen.resolve_descriptor(lcty)); // GETFIELD
            lgen.mv.visitVarInsn(store_opcode(lcty), lslot);
            lci = lci + 1;
        }
        let body_ty = lgen.gen_expr(lambda_body_copy);
        if m_ret == "Unit" {
            if body_ty != "Unit" {
                if body_ty == "Long" || body_ty == "Double" { lgen.mv.visitInsn(88); } else { lgen.mv.visitInsn(87); } // POP2 / POP
            }
            lgen.mv.visitInsn(177); // RETURN
        } else if is_ref_type(m_ret) {
            lgen.mv.visitInsn(176); // ARETURN
        } else if m_ret == "Long" {
            lgen.mv.visitInsn(173); // LRETURN
        } else if m_ret == "Double" {
            lgen.mv.visitInsn(175); // DRETURN
        } else if m_ret == "Float" {
            lgen.mv.visitInsn(174); // FRETURN
        } else {
            lgen.mv.visitInsn(172); // IRETURN
        }
        lgen.mv.visitMaxs(0, 0);
        lgen.mv.visitEnd();

        cw.visitEnd();
        CodegenShim::writeClass(cw, synth_name + ".class");

        // === Back at the CURRENT call site: construct the instance ===
        self.mv.visitTypeInsn(187, synth_name); // NEW
        self.mv.visitInsn(89); // DUP
        var ldi = 0;
        while ldi < captures.length() {
            let lcty2 = capture_types.get(ldi);
            self.mv.visitVarInsn(load_opcode(lcty2), self.slot_of(captures.get(ldi)));
            ldi = ldi + 1;
        }
        self.mv.visitMethodInsn(183, synth_name, "<init>", ctor_desc, false); // INVOKESPECIAL
        return target_type;
    }

    // `Type::method` used directly as a lambda-typed argument -- see `Checker.hotc`'s own
    // `check_method_ref` header for the full design. Builds the exact same synthetic
    // `Lambda { params: [mref_p0, ...], body: StaticCall { type_name, method, args } }` the
    // checker's own version builds and hands it straight to `gen_lambda`, unchanged -- no separate
    // emission path.
    fn gen_method_ref(&mut self, type_name: String, method: String, target_type: String) -> String {
        let iface_name = dyn_interface_name_cg(target_type);
        var m_count = 0;
        var ii2 = 0;
        while ii2 < self.interfaces_raw.length() {
            let ifd3 = self.interfaces_raw.get(ii2);
            let ifd4 = self.interfaces_raw.get(ii2); // fresh copy -- `.methods` is only safe to read once
            if ifd3.name == iface_name && ifd4.methods.length() == 1 {
                m_count = ifd4.methods.get(0).params.length();
            }
            ii2 = ii2 + 1;
        }
        let synth_params = synth_method_ref_lambda_params_cg(m_count);
        let synth_args = synth_method_ref_args_cg(&synth_params);
        let body = StaticCall { type_name: type_name, method: method, args: synth_args };
        return self.gen_lambda(synth_params, body, target_type);
    }

    // `Type::method` -- ambiguous between a genuine static FIELD read and a method-ref candidate,
    // see `Checker.hotc`'s own `check_args_lambda_aware` header for the disambiguation rule (real
    // declared field wins). Shared by every call site below that needs to decide whether an
    // `ExternStaticFieldGet` arg should go through `gen_method_ref` or the ordinary `gen_expr` path.
    fn is_method_ref_candidate_cg(&mut self, type_name: String, field: String) -> Bool {
        let ef_found = self.extern_field_type_names.get(type_name + "#" + field);
        var is_field = false;
        if let Some { value: efv } = ef_found { is_field = true; }
        return !is_field;
    }

    // See `Checker.hotc`'s own `resolve_named_args_chk` header -- identical reordering logic,
    // redone independently here against `self.fn_param_names` (this file's own registry, not
    // shared with the checker's).
    fn resolve_named_args_cg(&mut self, callee: String, args: Vec<Expr>) -> Vec<Expr> {
        var has_named = false;
        var has_positional = false;
        var i = 0;
        while i < args.length() {
            match args.get(i) {
                NamedArg { name, value } => { has_named = true; }
                _ => { has_positional = true; }
            }
            i = i + 1;
        }
        if !has_named { return args; }
        if has_positional {
            throw CodegenError::new("codegen: mixing named and positional arguments isn't supported in a call to '" + callee + "'");
        }
        let names_found = self.fn_param_names.get(callee);
        var declared = empty_str_vec4();
        var known = false;
        if let Some { value: dn } = names_found { declared = dn; known = true; }
        if !known {
            throw CodegenError::new("codegen: named arguments used in a call to '" + callee + "', but only plain top-level fn calls support named arguments this pass");
        }
        if declared.length() != args.length() {
            throw CodegenError::new("codegen: named-argument call to '" + callee + "' expects " + arity_str_cg(declared.length()) + " arg(s), got " + arity_str_cg(args.length()));
        }
        var reordered = empty_expr_vec_cg();
        var di = 0;
        while di < declared.length() {
            let pname = declared.get(di);
            var found_val = false;
            var val = IntLit { value: 0 };
            var ai = 0;
            while ai < args.length() {
                match args.get(ai) {
                    NamedArg { name, value } => {
                        if name == pname { found_val = true; val = value; }
                    }
                    _ => {}
                }
                ai = ai + 1;
            }
            if !found_val {
                throw CodegenError::new("codegen: named-argument call to '" + callee + "' is missing argument '" + pname + "'");
            }
            reordered.push(val);
            di = di + 1;
        }
        return reordered;
    }

    fn gen_variant_construct(&mut self, vowner: String, name: String, field_names: Vec<String>, field_values: Vec<Expr>) -> String {
        // Real, disclosed limitation found self-compiling this very function: the self-hosted
        // compiler's own `Match`/`if let` codegen (see `gen_stmt`'s own `Match` arm) only works
        // correctly when the JVM operand stack is EMPTY at the point it runs -- it stores its
        // subject into a fresh local slot and assumes returning to a zero-baseline afterward, an
        // invariant every ORDINARY HC statement already satisfies (statements always leave the
        // stack empty), but one this file's own manual `self.mv.visitInsn(89)` (`DUP`) calls
        // violate: they push real, un-tracked values onto the JVM stack directly, outside the
        // self-hosted compiler's own bookkeeping. Running an `if let Some { ... } = ... { ... }`
        // lookup WHILE a DUP'd instance ref sits on the stack produced a genuine ASM
        // `NegativeArraySizeException` in stack-frame merging (not caught by the self-hosted
        // checker, only by ASM's own `COMPUTE_FRAMES` at codegen time). Fixed by doing EVERY
        // `if`/`match`-requiring lookup (tag, per-field type resolution) UPFRONT, before any
        // bytecode emission at all -- the bytecode-emission phase below then uses only plain,
        // unconditional `visit*` calls, never interleaving control flow with a non-empty stack.
        let tag_key = vowner + "#" + name;
        var tag = 0;
        let tag_found = self.variant_tags.get(tag_key);
        if let Some { value: t } = tag_found {
            tag = t;
        }
        // Prefer THIS variant's own dedicated field table over `field_type_of(vowner, ...)`'s
        // enum-wide union (`Ast.hc`'s `Expr` enum reuses field names like "value" across variants
        // at different types; constructing e.g. `StringLit { value: s }` via the union table could
        // silently emit a `PUTFIELD` with the WRONG descriptor -- `IntLit`'s own `I`, say -- for a
        // real `String` value, a genuine `VerifyError`/miscompile, not just a wrong-message compile
        // error like the checker-side and `Match`-side versions of this bug were).
        let vfn_key2 = vowner + "#" + name;
        let vfn_found2 = self.variant_field_names.get(vfn_key2);
        let vft_found2 = self.variant_field_types.get(vfn_key2);
        var v2_fnames = empty_str_vec4();
        var v2_ftypes = empty_str_vec4();
        var has_v2_fields = false;
        if let Some { value: vfn2 } = vfn_found2 {
            if let Some { value: vft2 } = vft_found2 {
                v2_fnames = vfn2;
                v2_ftypes = vft2;
                has_v2_fields = true;
            }
        }
        var resolved_ftypes = empty_str_vec4();
        var pk = 0;
        while pk < field_names.length() {
            let fname0 = field_names.get(pk);
            var fty0 = self.field_type_of(vowner, fname0);
            if has_v2_fields {
                var v2i = 0;
                while v2i < v2_fnames.length() {
                    if v2_fnames.get(v2i) == fname0 {
                        fty0 = v2_ftypes.get(v2i);
                    }
                    v2i = v2i + 1;
                }
            }
            resolved_ftypes.push(fty0);
            pk = pk + 1;
        }
        // JVM owner arguments below need the real, module-qualified name -- `vowner` itself stays
        // bare everywhere else in this function (registry-key lookups, and the value returned to
        // the caller, which tracks the HC-level type name, not a JVM identifier).
        let qvowner = self.qualify(vowner);
        // A zero-field variant construction reads the real singleton `GETSTATIC` field
        // `gen_enum` emits for it, rather than allocating a fresh instance -- see that fn's own
        // header for why: real Java callers compare a returned value against
        // `EnumName.VARIANT` with genuine reference equality (`==`), which only works when every
        // construction of that variant, anywhere in the program (including from HC's OWN code,
        // not just Java-facing accessors), yields the exact same object.
        if field_names.length() == 0 {
            self.mv.visitFieldInsn(178, qvowner, name, "L" + qvowner + ";"); // GETSTATIC
            return vowner;
        }
        self.mv.visitTypeInsn(187, qvowner); // NEW
        self.mv.visitInsn(89); // DUP
        self.mv.visitMethodInsn(183, qvowner, "<init>", "()V", false); // INVOKESPECIAL -- stack: [instance]
        self.mv.visitInsn(89); // DUP -- stack: [instance, instance]
        self.mv.visitIntInsn(17, tag); // SIPUSH
        self.mv.visitFieldInsn(181, qvowner, "tag", "I"); // PUTFIELD -- stack: [instance]
        var k = 0;
        while k < field_names.length() {
            let fname = field_names.get(k);
            let fty = resolved_ftypes.get(k);
            self.mv.visitInsn(89); // DUP
            self.gen_expr(field_values.get(k));
            self.mv.visitFieldInsn(181, qvowner, name + "$" + fname, self.resolve_descriptor(fty)); // PUTFIELD -- mangled, see gen_enum's own header
            k = k + 1;
        }
        return vowner;
    }

    // Cheap, non-emitting type guess from an expression's own AST shape -- no `gen_expr` call, so
    // safe to use where the SAME expression gets `gen_expr`'d for real immediately afterward (see
    // `gen_expr`'s own `StructLit` generic-inference fallback) without emitting its bytecode twice.
    // Codegen-side mirror of `Checker.hotc`'s own `shape_only_ty` (reads `self.var_types` here
    // instead of the checker's `self.vars` -- this file's own local-type registry).
    fn shape_only_ty_cg(&mut self, e: &Expr) -> String {
        match e {
            IntLit { value } => { return "Int"; }
            StringLit { value } => { return "String"; }
            BoolLit { value } => { return "Bool"; }
            Ident { name } => {
                let v = self.lookup_type(name);
                if let Some { value: ty } = v { return ty; }
                return "?";
            }
            // Mirrors `Checker.hotc`'s own identical `shape_only_ty` fix (this file doesn't share
            // that registry, same "duplicated independently" split as everywhere else) -- a NESTED
            // struct literal's own `name` field IS its type name for the common, non-generic case.
            StructLit { name, field_names, field_values, generic_base } => { return name; }
            // Mirrors `Checker.hotc`'s own identical `shape_only_ty` fix, same reasoning --
            // `field_type_of` isn't reused directly since it THROWS on an unknown field/struct
            // (the right behavior for real codegen, wrong for this "best-effort guess" fn, which
            // needs a plain "?" on any miss instead).
            FieldAccess { obj, field } => {
                let ot = self.shape_only_ty_cg(obj);
                if ot == "?" { return "?"; }
                let names_found = self.struct_fields.get(ot);
                let types_found = self.struct_ftypes.get(ot);
                if let Some { value: names } = names_found {
                    if let Some { value: types } = types_found {
                        var i = 0;
                        while i < names.length() {
                            if names.get(i) == field {
                                return types.get(i);
                            }
                            i = i + 1;
                        }
                    }
                }
                return "?";
            }
            _ => { return "?"; }
        }
    }

    // Emits the bytecode for `e` and returns the "Int"/"Bool"/"String" type tag of the value it
    // just pushed -- see this file's own header for why this walk computes the tag inline instead
    // of reading it off the (type-less) AST node itself.
    fn gen_expr(&mut self, e: &Expr) -> String {
        match e {
            // Real bug found stress-testing `Long` arithmetic (an unrelated literal like
            // `300000` in the SAME expression): `SIPUSH`'s own operand is a SIGNED 16-BIT value
            // (-32768..32767), but this arm used it UNCONDITIONALLY for every `Int` literal --
            // anything outside that range silently wrapped (`300000` became `-27680`, no error,
            // no warning). Latent all session since no earlier demo happened to use a literal that
            // large. Fixed by falling back to a real `LDC` (via a host-side `Integer.valueOf`
            // boxing call, see `JIntegerCg`'s own header) for anything `SIPUSH` can't represent.
            IntLit { value } => {
                if value >= -32768 && value <= 32767 {
                    self.mv.visitIntInsn(17, value); // SIPUSH
                } else {
                    let boxed = JIntegerCg::valueOf(value);
                    self.mv.visitLdcInsn(boxed as JObject4);
                }
                return "Int";
            }
            // `java.lang.Float`/`java.lang.Double`'s own static `valueOf(String)` parses AND boxes
            // in one real JVM call -- no separate `parseFloat`/`parseDouble` + manual boxing needed
            // (mirrors the `Integer.valueOf(int)` boxing call `IntLit`'s own large-constant branch,
            // right above, already uses -- same "hand ASM's real `visitLdcInsn(Object)` a real Java
            // object" mechanism, see that extern class's own header). `Lexer.hotc`'s own `number()`
            // already stripped the `f`/`F` suffix, so `text` here is always a plain parseable
            // decimal (`"3.5"`, never `"3.5f"`).
            FloatLit { text } => {
                let boxed = JFloatCg::valueOf(text);
                self.mv.visitLdcInsn(boxed as JObject4);
                return "Float";
            }
            DoubleLit { text } => {
                let boxed2 = JDoubleCg::valueOf(text);
                self.mv.visitLdcInsn(boxed2 as JObject4);
                return "Double";
            }
            BoolLit { value } => {
                if value { self.mv.visitInsn(4); } else { self.mv.visitInsn(3); } // ICONST_1 / ICONST_0
                return "Bool";
            }
            StringLit { value } => { self.mv.visitLdcInsn(value as JObject4); return "String"; }
            StringInterp { literals, exprs } => { return self.gen_string_interp(literals, exprs); }
            Ident { name } => {
                // A LOCAL (`var_types` -- params, `let`/`var` bindings, match-arm field bindings)
                // is checked first, so a local can still shadow a top-level `static` of the same
                // name. Only when there's no local by this name do we check `static_types` and
                // emit `GETSTATIC` instead -- see `Ast.hc`'s own `StaticDecl` header.
                let local_found = self.lookup_type(name);
                if let Some { value: lty } = local_found {
                    let slot = self.slot_of(name);
                    self.mv.visitVarInsn(load_opcode(lty), slot);
                    return lty;
                }
                let static_found = self.static_types.get(name);
                if let Some { value: sty } = static_found {
                    self.mv.visitFieldInsn(178, self.owner, name, self.resolve_descriptor(sty)); // GETSTATIC
                    return sty;
                }
                // Neither a local nor a static -- a BARE fieldless-enum-variant reference used as
                // an ordinary value expression (`RETURN`, no `{}` at all -- real `Lexer.hotc`/
                // `Parser.hotc` usage, e.g. `Token { kind: FN }`/`kind == RETURN`, treating a
                // fieldless enum's variants like plain named constants). Only bare, non-generic
                // variants resolve this way (`variant_owner`'s own bare-name table -- see `Ast.hc`'s
                // own `EnumDecl` header for why a generic enum's variants can't use this form),
                // reusing the exact same construction bytecode `StructLit`'s own variant-
                // construction arm uses, just with no fields supplied (`gen_variant_construct`).
                let variant_found = self.variant_owner.get(name);
                if let Some { value: vowner2 } = variant_found {
                    return self.gen_variant_construct(vowner2, name, empty_str_vec4(), empty_expr_vec_cg());
                }
                // A bare GENERIC-enum fieldless variant used as a value ("None", real
                // `Parser.hotc` usage) -- mirrors `StructLit`'s own generic-variant fallback right
                // above in this file, and `Checker.hotc`'s `check_ident`, same real gap.
                if is_mono_name_cg(self.ret_ty) {
                    // `self.variant_tags` is keyed by the FULL mono enum name ("Option$Expr#Some",
                    // see `build_program_info`'s own `mono_names` loop) -- `self.ret_ty` already
                    // IS that full mono name, so the key is just `self.ret_ty + "#" + name`
                    // directly, no `mono_base_cg` involved (an earlier version of this fix wrongly
                    // stripped it down to "Option#Some", which is never a real registered key --
                    // caused a genuine ASM `Frame.pop` crash, found self-compiling `Parser.hotc`).
                    let tag_key2 = self.ret_ty + "#" + name;
                    let tag_found2 = self.variant_tags.get(tag_key2);
                    if let Some { value: tag2 } = tag_found2 {
                        return self.gen_variant_construct(self.ret_ty, name, empty_str_vec4(), empty_expr_vec_cg());
                    }
                }
                let ty = self.type_of(name);
                let slot = self.slot_of(name);
                self.mv.visitVarInsn(load_opcode(ty), slot);
                return ty;
            }
            Binary { op, left, right } => { return self.gen_binary(op, left, right); }
            Unary { op, expr } => { return self.gen_unary(op, expr); }
            Call { callee, args } => {
                // `createEnemy(health: 100, damage: 20)` -- reordered back to ordinary positional
                // order right here, before anything else in this arm runs, so every line below
                // sees exactly the same `args` shape an ordinary positional call always has. See
                // `Checker.hotc`'s own `resolve_named_args_chk` header -- this is its codegen
                // mirror (independent registries, same split as everywhere else in this file); a
                // real error there already blocks compilation before codegen ever reaches this
                // point for anything this mirror can't also resolve.
                let args = self.resolve_named_args_cg(callee, args);
                // The real intrinsic `print(...)` (never a real user `FnDecl`, so it was NEVER
                // going to be found in `fn_descs` below -- `Checker.hotc`'s own `check_call`
                // already tolerates it, not via any `print`-specific case but its general "unknown
                // callee -> trust it, return TyUnknown" fallback, the same honesty `extern class`
                // gets) -- a real, disclosed gap found running
                // `run_selfcompile_stage2`: `Driver.hotc`'s own driving logic prints constantly,
                // and nothing before this ever exercised compiling a `print(...)` call THROUGH
                // this self-hosted codegen at all (the six core compiler files are pure library
                // code with no printing of their own). Mirrors the real compiler's own `CodeGen.
                // kt` `genCall` exactly: `GETSTATIC System.out`, evaluate the one argument, then
                // `println` with the descriptor picked by the ARGUMENT's own runtime type -- Int/
                // Bool/Long get their own primitive overload, everything else (String, and any
                // ref type with a real `toString`-like representation) falls back to the `String`
                // overload.
                if callee == "print" {
                    self.mv.visitFieldInsn(178, "java/lang/System", "out", "Ljava/io/PrintStream;"); // GETSTATIC
                    let arg_ty = self.gen_expr(args.get(0));
                    var desc = "(Ljava/lang/String;)V";
                    if arg_ty == "Int" { desc = "(I)V"; }
                    if arg_ty == "Bool" { desc = "(Z)V"; }
                    if arg_ty == "Long" { desc = "(J)V"; }
                    if arg_ty == "Float" { desc = "(F)V"; }
                    if arg_ty == "Double" { desc = "(D)V"; }
                    if arg_ty == "Char" { desc = "(C)V"; }
                    // `PrintStream` has no real `println(byte)` overload -- real Java itself
                    // auto-widens a `byte` argument to `int` at the CALL SITE for exactly this
                    // reason, so `(I)V` (the value's already sitting on the stack as a plain int)
                    // is the correct, real-Java-equivalent descriptor, not a workaround.
                    if arg_ty == "Byte" { desc = "(I)V"; }
                    self.mv.visitMethodInsn(182, "java/io/PrintStream", "println", desc, false); // INVOKEVIRTUAL
                    return "Unit";
                }
                // Three more real intrinsics, same "never a real `FnDecl`, `Checker.hotc`'s own
                // `check_call` already tolerates them via its unknown-callee fallback" story as
                // `print` right above -- `$stdin` is a real static `BufferedReader` field every
                // compiled class now carries (see `compile_program`'s own class-header emission
                // and `gen_static_init`'s own header), initialized once in `<clinit>` exactly like
                // `System.out` is already implicitly available. Mirrors the real compiler's own
                // `Codegen.kt` exactly: `read_line` is the ONLY real codegen-level intrinsic --
                // `read_int`/`read_string` are ordinary real HC functions (parsing `read_line()`'s
                // own result, `try`/`catch`ing `NumberFormatException`), injected via this file's
                // own `prelude_source()` in `Driver.hotc`, same as the real compiler's own
                // `Prelude.kt` -- see that fn's own header. A first draft of this feature
                // special-cased `read_int` as a THIRD codegen intrinsic directly here (a raw
                // `Integer.parseInt` with no error handling) before finding `Prelude.kt`'s own real
                // shape (`Option<Int>`, `None` on a bad line) -- `examples/calc/Calculator.hotc`'s
                // own `if let Option::Some { value: a } = read_int() { ... }` usage is what caught
                // the mismatch.
                if callee == "read_line" {
                    self.mv.visitFieldInsn(178, self.owner, "$stdin", "Ljava/io/BufferedReader;"); // GETSTATIC
                    self.mv.visitMethodInsn(182, "java/io/BufferedReader", "readLine", "()Ljava/lang/String;", false); // INVOKEVIRTUAL
                    return "String";
                }
                // Same-class static call only this phase -- every `FnDecl` this codegen ever
                // sees was compiled into `self.owner` by `compile_program` below, so there's no
                // cross-class or cross-package call target to resolve, unlike a real `extern
                // class` call site. Anything else (a real builtin like `print`, which the checker
                // happily accepts as `TyUnknown` -- see `Checker.hotc`'s own `check_call`) would
                // previously silently emit an `INVOKESTATIC` to a method that doesn't exist,
                // failing at class-verify time with a confusing `NoSuchMethodError`. Looking the
                // callee up in `fn_descs` turns that into a clear, immediate compile-time error,
                // and gives the *real* descriptor (built from the callee's actual declared
                // param/return types -- see this file's own header) instead of guessing `Int` for
                // everything.
                let found = self.fn_descs.get(callee);
                var known = false;
                if let Some { value: d } = found {
                    known = true;
                }
                // A callee not found directly might be a generic fn template's own name (`callee`
                // was never registered into `fn_descs` -- see `build_program_info`'s own header)
                // -- `generic_fn_infer_idx` names which of ITS params carries the inference (the
                // one whose declared type is exactly the bare type parameter, see `Checker.hotc`'s
                // own `check_generic_call`), read back here independently since this file doesn't
                // share the checker's own registries. Args are evaluated in normal left-to-right
                // order regardless (needed for the call either way); the inferred index's own
                // return type (captured as `gen_expr` pushes it, not by peeking ahead) is what
                // decides the concrete mono name -- no reordering needed, since which METHOD gets
                // invoked is only decided at the final `INVOKESTATIC`, after every arg is already
                // on the stack.
                var infer_idx = -1;
                var infer_unwrap = 0;
                var is_generic_call = false;
                // `fn hash_map2_new<K: Hashable, V>(...)` -- the SECOND type param's own
                // inference index, mirroring `infer_idx`/`infer_unwrap` exactly (see `Ast.hc`'s
                // own `FnDecl.type_param2` header, and `generic_fn_infer_idx2`'s own header for
                // why this file needs its own independent copy of this metadata).
                var infer_idx2 = -1;
                var infer_unwrap2 = 0;
                if !known {
                    let idx_found = self.generic_fn_infer_idx.get(callee);
                    if let Some { value: idx } = idx_found {
                        infer_idx = idx;
                        is_generic_call = true;
                        let unwrap_found = self.generic_fn_infer_unwrap.get(callee);
                        if let Some { value: uw } = unwrap_found { infer_unwrap = uw; }
                        let idx2_found = self.generic_fn_infer_idx2.get(callee);
                        if let Some { value: idx2 } = idx2_found {
                            infer_idx2 = idx2;
                            let unwrap2_found = self.generic_fn_infer_unwrap2.get(callee);
                            if let Some { value: uw2 } = unwrap2_found { infer_unwrap2 = uw2; }
                        }
                    }
                }
                if !known && !is_generic_call {
                    throw CodegenError::new("codegen for 'Call' to '" + callee + "' not implemented this phase (only calls to functions defined in the same source file are supported)");
                }
                let argc = args.length();
                var inferred_type = "";
                var inferred_type2 = "";
                // `known` (a real, non-generic top-level fn) is the only case a `Lambda` arg can
                // target here -- a generic call's own inference (right above) has no lambda support
                // wired up (a real, disclosed scope cut matching `Checker.hotc`'s own
                // `check_call`/`backfill_lambda_args_ty`, which only backfills for the SAME `known`
                // branch there too).
                if known && args_has_lambda_cg(&args) {
                    var call_param_types = empty_str_vec4();
                    let cpt_found = self.fn_param_types.get(callee);
                    if let Some { value: cpt } = cpt_found { call_param_types = cpt; }
                    var i = 0;
                    while i < argc {
                        let a3 = args.get(i);
                        match a3 {
                            Lambda { params, body } => {
                                var target_ty3 = "";
                                if i < call_param_types.length() { target_ty3 = call_param_types.get(i); }
                                self.gen_lambda(params, body, target_ty3);
                            }
                            ExternStaticFieldGet { type_name, field } => {
                                if self.is_method_ref_candidate_cg(type_name, field) {
                                    var target_ty3b = "";
                                    if i < call_param_types.length() { target_ty3b = call_param_types.get(i); }
                                    self.gen_method_ref(type_name, field, target_ty3b);
                                } else {
                                    self.gen_expr(a3);
                                }
                            }
                            _ => { self.gen_expr(a3); }
                        }
                        i = i + 1;
                    }
                } else {
                    var i = 0;
                    while i < argc {
                        let at = self.gen_expr(args.get(i));
                        if is_generic_call && i == infer_idx {
                            // `b: Box<T>` -- see `ProgramInfo`'s own header on
                            // `generic_fn_infer_unwrap`: `at` here is the ARG's own real mono name
                            // (`"Box$Int"`), one level deeper than the concrete type argument `T`
                            // itself actually needs to be.
                            if infer_unwrap == 1 && is_mono_name_cg(at) {
                                inferred_type = mono_arg_cg(at);
                            } else if infer_unwrap == 2 && is_array_type_name_cg(at) {
                                // `arr: &[T]` -- see the `infer_unwrap` computation's own header,
                                // right above in `build_program_info`.
                                inferred_type = array_elem_type_name_cg(at);
                            } else {
                                inferred_type = at;
                            }
                        }
                        if is_generic_call && infer_idx2 >= 0 && i == infer_idx2 {
                            if infer_unwrap2 == 1 && is_mono_name_cg(at) {
                                inferred_type2 = mono_arg_cg(at);
                            } else if infer_unwrap2 == 2 && is_array_type_name_cg(at) {
                                inferred_type2 = array_elem_type_name_cg(at);
                            } else {
                                inferred_type2 = at;
                            }
                        }
                        i = i + 1;
                    }
                }
                var target = callee;
                if is_generic_call {
                    if inferred_type == "" {
                        throw CodegenError::new("codegen: could not infer generic type argument for call to '" + callee + "'");
                    }
                    target = callee + "$" + inferred_type;
                    if infer_idx2 >= 0 {
                        if inferred_type2 == "" {
                            throw CodegenError::new("codegen: could not infer second generic type argument for call to '" + callee + "'");
                        }
                        target = target + "$" + inferred_type2;
                    }
                }
                let desc_found = self.fn_descs.get(target);
                var desc = "";
                if let Some { value: d2 } = desc_found { desc = d2; }
                self.mv.visitMethodInsn(184, self.owner, target, desc, false); // INVOKESTATIC
                let rt_found = self.fn_ret_tys.get(target);
                if let Some { value: rt } = rt_found {
                    return rt;
                }
                return "Int";
            }
            FieldAccess { obj, field } => {
                let ot = self.gen_expr(obj);
                return self.finish_field_access(ot, field);
            }
            MethodCall { recv, method, args } => {
                let rt = self.gen_expr(recv);
                return self.finish_method_call(rt, method, args);
            }
            Assign { name, value } => {
                let ty = self.gen_expr(value);
                // A LOCAL (`var_types`) is checked first, so a local still shadows a top-level
                // `static` of the same name -- only falls back to `PUTSTATIC` when there's no
                // local by this name, mirroring `Ident`'s own read-side local-then-static
                // precedence right above. Real gap found needing genuine mutable module-level
                // state (a stateful "manager" ported from Java, `private static` fields
                // reassigned across calls): every `pub static` was previously write-once
                // (`<clinit>` only) -- `slot_of` silently returns slot 0 for an unknown name
                // rather than erroring, so assigning to a static SILENTLY CORRUPTED whatever
                // local (often `self`/the first param) happened to live in slot 0, instead of
                // failing loudly. See `Checker.hotc`'s own `check_assign` header for the
                // matching type-check half of this fix, which now guarantees `name` really is a
                // registered static by the time codegen ever sees this, for any program that
                // passed checking.
                let local_found3 = self.lookup_type(name);
                if let Some { value: _lty3 } = local_found3 {
                    let slot = self.slot_of(name);
                    self.mv.visitVarInsn(store_opcode(ty), slot);
                    self.mv.visitVarInsn(load_opcode(ty), slot); // push the stored value back so
                    // `Assign` behaves like every other value-producing expr (matching real
                    // assignment-as-expression semantics), letting `ExprStmt` codegen stay uniform
                    // below instead of needing to special-case Assign to know whether a value is on
                    // the stack to pop.
                    return ty;
                }
                // `DUP`/`DUP2` (category-2 for `Long`/`Double`) keeps one copy on the stack for
                // `PUTSTATIC` to consume and leaves the other as this expression's own value,
                // mirroring the local case's store-then-reload semantics without a redundant
                // `GETSTATIC` read back.
                if ty == "Long" || ty == "Double" {
                    self.mv.visitInsn(92); // DUP2
                } else {
                    self.mv.visitInsn(89); // DUP
                }
                self.mv.visitFieldInsn(179, self.owner, name, self.resolve_descriptor(ty)); // PUTSTATIC
                return ty;
            }
            FieldAssign { obj, field, value } => {
                return self.finish_field_assign(self.gen_expr(obj), field, value);
            }
            StructLit { name, field_names, field_values, generic_base } => {
                // Enum variant construction (`VariantName { ... }`) takes a different bytecode
                // shape than a plain struct literal: the *enum's* class (not a per-variant one --
                // there is only ever one real `.class` file per enum, see `gen_enum`) with a
                // trivial no-arg constructor, `tag` and each field set via individual `PUTFIELD`s
                // afterward rather than one big constructor call (different variant literals
                // populate different subsets of the enum's unioned fields). An explicitly-qualified
                // generic-enum-variant construction (`Option<Int>::Some { ... }`) already names its
                // exact concrete instantiation via `generic_base` -- skip the bare-name
                // `variant_owner` lookup entirely (it would collide across multiple concrete
                // instantiations of the same generic enum; see `Ast.hc`'s own `EnumDecl` header).
                var vowner = "";
                var is_variant = false;
                if let Some { value: gb } = generic_base {
                    vowner = gb;
                    is_variant = true;
                } else {
                    let owner_found = self.variant_owner.get(name);
                    if let Some { value: o } = owner_found {
                        vowner = o;
                        is_variant = true;
                    } else {
                        // A bare GENERIC-enum variant ("Some { ... }" / "None", no `<Arg>` and no
                        // owner registered -- `build_program_info`'s own header explains why a
                        // mono generic enum instantiation deliberately skips `variant_owner_*`).
                        // Falls back to `self.ret_ty` the same way the plain-struct branch below
                        // does (mirrors `Checker.hotc`'s own `check_struct_lit` fix, same real gap
                        // found self-compiling `Parser.hotc`'s `some_expr`/bare-`None` helpers):
                        // if `name` is a real variant of the enum `self.ret_ty` names, that's it.
                        if is_mono_name_cg(self.ret_ty) {
